\p
\v 36 Yahweh¦343762 said¦343761 to me, “Humanity's child, will you¦343767 judge¦343767 Oholah¦343770 and Oholibah¦343773? Present them \add a list of\add* their¦343777 disgusting actions,
\v 37 since they've committed adultery, and also have blood¦343781 on¦343782 their hands¦343782. They've committed adultery \add ≈by worshipping\add* their idols, and they've even caused their sons to pass through¦343795 the fire \add to be considered\add* as food for their idols.
\v 38 Then \add ≈on top of that\add*, they defile my sanctuary¦343806, and \add ≈at the same time\add*, they disrespect my rest days.
\v 39 When¦343814 they'd slaughtered¦343814 their children¦343817 for their idols¦343818, then¦343814 they came¦343819 to my sanctuary¦343822 on¦343814 the same day¦343823 to desecrate it¦343825. So¦343814 listen, that's what they've done \add ≈right there in¦343814 my residence\add*.
\p
\v 40 You sent messengers¦343840 \add to go and bring\add* men¦343836 from far away. Now look, they indeed came¦343845—those¦343846 who you \add prepared for by\add* bathing, painting¦343848 your eyes¦343849, and adorning¦343850 yourself¦343850 with jewellery.
\v 41 You sat¦343853 on a lovely couch¦343856 bed with a table¦343858 arranged¦343859 \add ≈in front of you\add* where you placed my incense¦343861 and¦343853 my \add olive\add* oil¦343862.
\v 42 Then¦343866 the sound¦343866 of a noisy crowd¦343867 was around her¦343869 as all kinds of men¦343872, even Sabeans¦343876, were brought¦343875 in¦343869 from the wilderness¦343878, and they¦343879 put bracelets¦343880 on their hands¦343883 and beautiful¦343885 crowns¦343884 on their heads¦343888.
\v 43 Then¦343890 I said¦343890 about \add ≈that experienced adulterer¦343892\add*, ‘Now they'll \add ≈treat her¦343897 as a prostitute¦343895\add*, and¦343898 she'll fornicate¦343897 with them.’
\v 44 They \add ≈had sex with\add* her like men do with a prostitute¦343906. In that way they¦343908 \add ≈had sex with\add* the two immoral women¦343915, Oholah¦343911 and¦343912 Oholibah¦343914.
\v 45 \add ≈But¦343918\add* righteous¦343919 men will \add ≈judge¦343921\add* and punish them as adulteresses, and they'll punish them with¦343932 the \add appropriate\add* sentence for those who've shed blood, because¦343928 they're adulteresses plus¦343925 they have blood on¦343932 their¦343932 hands¦343932.
\p
\v 46 So the master¦343938 Yahweh¦343938 says¦343937 this: I'll bring¦343940 a group¦343942 \add to attack\add* \add @Shomron and Yerushalem\add* and give them up to be terrorized¦343945 and plundered¦343946.
\v 47 Then¦343948 that group¦343951 will stone¦343948 them with stones and cut¦343952 them down with their swords¦343954. They'll kill¦343957 their \add ≈children\add* and burn¦343960 down their houses¦343958.
\v 48 \add That's how\add* I'll¦343962 remove shameful behaviour from the land, and¦343962 discipline all the women¦343970 so they'll no longer act like prostitutes.
\v 49 They'll use your shameful behaviour against you. You'll all bear the guilt of the sins¦343978 you did with your idols¦343979, and¦343975 in that way you'll know¦343981 that I am the master¦343984 Yahweh¦343984.”
\c 24
\s1 The rusty cooking pot
\rem /s1 The Corroded Cooking Pot; The Sign of the Cooking Pot; The Cooking Pot; The siege and capture of Jerusalem; The Boiling Pot
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦343991 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦343989 on¦343993 the 15th of January of the ninth¦343994 year¦343993 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*.
\v 2 “Humanity's child, write¦344004 down \add ≈the day and month\add* for yourself¦344007, because on¦344024 this exact day, the Babylonian king \add ≈started his siege of\add* Yerushalem¦344023.\x + \xo 24:2: \xt 2Ki 25:1.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈So¦344028 tell this parable¦344034 to the rebellious¦344033 Israeli people and explain that it comes from\add* the master¦344039 Yahweh¦344039:
\q1 Pour water¦344048 into the cooking pot and place it on the fire.
\q1
\v 4 Put in the leg and shoulder¦344058 of a good¦344056 \add sheep\add*,
\q1 then fill¦344061 it up with the best bones¦344060.
\q1
\v 5 \add Yes,\add* take¦344065 the best one from the flock¦344064
\q1 and pile up the \add ≈fuel\add* underneath¦344069 it—
\q1 keeping it boiling¦344071 to \add make¦344070 sure\add* the bones in¦344076 it cook \add well\add*.
\p
\v 6 \add ≈Now\add* the master¦344084 Yahweh¦344084 says¦344082 this: The city¦344087 of blood¦344088 \add ≈won't end well\add*—\add it's like\add* a cooking pot that has rust in¦344092 it, and that rust won't come out of it. Take piece after piece out of it, but¦344093 don't¦344094 \add ≈be choosy about which¦344090 pieces\add*,
\v 7 because \add ≈the blood¦344107 the city shed\add* is \add all the way\add* \add ≈through\add* its¦344108 centre. It \add ≈displayed the blood\add* on a flat rock¦344113 \add to be easily seen\add*, rather than pouring it out on the ground where it could be covered¦344120 over with¦344108 dust,
\v 8 \add ≈so it makes me angry enough to\add* get revenge¦344127: I¦344128 placed \em the city's\em* blood¦344131 on the smooth rock¦344135, so it couldn't be covered¦344137.
\p
\v 9 Therefore, the master¦344143 Yahweh¦344143 says¦344142 this: The city¦344146 of blood¦344147 \add ≈won't end well\add*. I'll¦344151 also enlarge the pile of \add fire\add*wood \add to put underneath it\add*.
\v 10 \add ≈Stack up\add* the wood and kindle¦344156 the fire¦344157. \add ≈Cook the meat well\add* and mix in the spices¦344161 and let the bones¦344162 be charred¦344163.
\v 11 Then¦344165 put the empty¦344169 pot onto \add the hot\add* coals¦344168 in order to heat and¦344165 scorch its bronze¦344173, so the impurities in it will be melted¦344174—its corrosion \add ≈eaten away\add*.’
\v 12 \add ?≈That was a lot of work, but¦344182\add* the fire¦344188 didn't¦344182 remove the corrosion from it.
\v 13 Your shameful behaviour is in your uncleanness. Because you wouldn't \add ≈accept it when\add* I tried to cleanse¦344194 you, you won't¦344195 be purified anymore until¦344202 I've satisfied¦344204 my fury against¦344201 you.
\v 14 I, Yahweh, have declared it, and I will do it. I won't¦344215 relent, nor will I rest from it. \add ≈You'll be judged¦344225 by your own practices¦344223 and your own behaviour\add*. That's the master¦344227 Yahweh's¦344211 declaration.”
\s1 The death of Yehezkel's wife
\rem /s1 The Death of the Prophet's Wife; The Death of Ezekiel's Wife; Ezekiel's Bereavement; Ezekiel's Wife Dies
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh¦344234 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦344232:
\v 16 “Listen, humanity's¦344240 child, I'm taking your \add ≈cherished wife\add* from you with¦344248 a plague, but¦344249 you mustn't mourn¦344252 or weep, or let any tears¦344255 \add ≈show\add*.
\v 17 You must¦344269 groan silently¦344259. Don't¦344262 \add ≈organise\add* a funeral \add ≈when she's\add* dead¦344260. Put on¦344270 your turban¦344265 and your sandals¦344268, but¦344268 don't¦344262 veil your beard, or eat¦344279 the bread¦344276 of men who mourn¦344261 who've lost their wives.”
\p
\v 18 \add ≈So¦344281\add* I spoke¦344281 to the people¦344284 in the morning, and¦344281 then¦344281 my wife¦344287 died¦344286 in the evening¦344288. In the morning I did¦344289 what I'd been commanded¦344292 to do.
\v 19 Then¦344294 the people¦344296 asked me, “Won't you tell us what these¦344303 things mean—the \add ≈way that you're¦344306 behaving\add*?”
\p
\v 20 “Yahweh¦344313 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦344311,” I answered them,
\v 21 “to tell the \add ≈Israeli¦344320 people\add* that the master¦344325 Yahweh¦344325 says¦344324 this to them: Listen, I'll desecrate my sanctuary¦344331—the pride¦344332 of your power¦344333, the delight of your eyes¦344335, and the yearning of your soul¦344337—and \add also\add* your sons¦344338 and daughters¦344339 who you¦344341 left behind \add there in¦344342 Yerushalem\add* will be killed by the sword¦344342.
\v 22 \add ≈When¦344345 that happens,\add* you \add people¦344354\add* will do¦344345 exactly as I've¦344347 done¦344347: you won't¦344351 veil your beards, or eat¦344356 the bread¦344353 of mourning men,
\v 23 \add ≈but¦344358 instead\add* your turbans¦344358 will be on your heads¦344361, and your sandals¦344362 on your feet¦344363. You won't¦344364 mourn¦344367 or weep, because you'll melt away \add ≈due to your disobedience\add*, and everyone will groan¦344370 for \add ≈their relatives\add*.
\v 24 \add ≈So¦344376\add* Yehezkel will be a sign¦344379 for you, because¦344387 when that time comes¦344385, you'll do¦344384 \add ≈exactly the same as he's doing\add*. Then¦344376 you \add people\add* will know¦344386 that I am the master¦344389 Yahweh¦344389.’ ”
\p
\v 25 “But¦344393 you, humanity's¦344396 child, on the day¦344398 that I capture their temple, (which is their joy¦344404, their pride, and what they see and desire¦344408), and when I take¦344399 away their sons¦344414 and daughters¦344415,
\v 26 a refugee will come¦344419 to you here on that day¦344417 to give you the news¦344422.
\v 27 At that time, your¦344428 mouth¦344428 will be opened¦344427 up to that refugee and you'll speak¦344432 \add again¦344435\add*—you'll no longer be silent. You'll be a sign¦344438 for them so that they'll know¦344439 that I am Yahweh¦344443.”
\c 25
\ms1 Yahweh's judgement of nations
\mr (25:1–32:32)
\s1 Ammon's punishment
\rem /s1 Proclamation against Ammon; Prophecy against Ammon; A Prophecy Against Ammon; Prophecies against foreign nations; A Message for Ammon
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦344449 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦344447:\x + \xo 25:1-7: \xt Jer 49:1-6; Eze 21:28-32; Amos 1:13-15; Zeph 2:8-11.\x*
\v 2 “Humanity's child, \add ≈keep looking towards the Ammonites\add* and¦344462 prophesy¦344462 against¦344458 them.
\v 3 Tell \add *them\add* to listen¦344468 to this message¦344469 \add because¦344478\add* this is what the master Yahweh¦344471 says: You \add ≈celebrated\add* my sanctuary¦344483 \add ≈being\add* desecrated, and the land¦344489 of Yisrael¦344490 \add ≈being\add* desolate¦344492, and the \add ≈kingdom\add* of Yehudah¦344496 \add ≈going\add* into exile¦344499,
\v 4 so \add ≈because of that\add*, listen: I'm giving you to a people¦344504 in¦344512 the east as their possession¦344507. They'll set up camp against you and set up their tents¦344513 among¦344512 you. They'll eat¦344515 your fruit¦344516 and drink¦344518 your milk¦344519.
\v 5 I'll¦344521 make¦344521 \add the Ammonite capital city\add*, Rabbah,\f + \fr 25:5 \ft Now the modern city of Amman (in Jordan) has been built on Rabbah's ruins.\f* into a camel¦344526 pasture¦344525, and¦344521 the Ammonite¦344530 people¦344529 into a resting¦344531 place¦344531 for flocks¦344533. Then¦344521 you \add people\add* will know¦344534 that I am Yahweh¦344538.
\p
\v 6 Because the master¦344544 Yahweh¦344544 says¦344543 this: You clapped¦344547 your hands and stamped¦344549 your feet¦344550 and celebrated with¦344550 all the contempt within you¦344547 against the \add ≈Israeli¦344558 nation\add*,
\v 7 therefore, listen \add Ammon\add*. I'll strike you \add ≈down\add* and give¦344568 you as plunder¦344570 to the \add other\add* nations¦344573. I'll cut¦344574 you off from the people¦344577 groups and make you perish¦344578 from among the countries¦344581. I'll destroy¦344582 you, and you'll know¦344583 that I am Yahweh¦344587.’
\s1 Moab's punishment
\rem /s1 A Prophecy Against Moab; Proclamation against Moab; A Message for Moab; Prophecy against Moab
\p
\v 8 The master¦344592 Yahweh¦344592 says¦344591 this, ‘Because Moab¦344596 and¦344597 Seir¦344597 say that the \add ≈kingdom\add* of Yehudah¦344603 is \add just\add* like every other nation¦344601,\x + \xo 25:8-11: \xt Gen 19:36-37; 25:10-12; Jer 48:1-47; Amos 2:1-3; Zeph 2:8-11.\x*
\v 9 \add ≈well\add* listen, I'll \add ≈destroy the border¦344614 cities\add* \add protecting\add* Moab¦344611, starting with the splendour of Beyt-Yeshimot, Baal¦344619-Meon¦344620, and Kiriataim¦344621.
\v 10 \add I'll hand them over\add* to the people in¦344640 the east who've \add already\add* been against the Ammonites—I'll give them up as a possession¦344632 so that the Ammonites won't¦344634 be remembered¦344636 among¦344640 the nations¦344640.
\v 11 \add ≈Yes,\add* I'll¦344643 execute judgements¦344644 against Moab, then¦344642 they'll know¦344645 that I am Yahweh¦344649.’
\s1 Edom's punishment
\rem /s1 A Prophecy Against Edom; Prophecy against Edom; Proclamation against Edom; A Message for Edom
\p
\v 12 The¦344655 master¦344654 Yahweh¦344654 \add also\add* says¦344653, ‘Edom¦344658 has taken vengeance¦344660 against¦344661 the \add ≈kingdom\add* of Yehudah¦344662 and has done wrong by doing that.\x + \xo 25:12-14: \xt Gen 25:24-34; 36:39; Jer 49:7-22; Eze 35:1-15; Jer 49:7-22; Oba 1-14; Mal 1:2-5.\x*
\v 13 Therefore, the master¦344671 Yahweh¦344671 says¦344670: I'll hit Edom¦344677 with¦344686 my hand¦344674 and destroy every person and animal¦344681 there. I'll make¦344682 it a ruined, abandoned place, from Teman¦344684 \add all the way\add* to Dedan¦344685—they'll be killed by the sword¦344686.
\v 14 I'll¦344689 \add ≈use\add* my people¦344695 Yisrael¦344696 to place my vengeance on¦344693 Edom, and they'll \add ≈apply\add* my anger¦344699 and fury to Edom \add ≈so\add* they'll know¦344701 my vengeance. That's the master¦344706 Yahweh's¦344707 declaration.’
\s1 Philistia's punishment
\rem /s1 A Prophecy Against Philistia; Prophecy against Philistia; Proclamation against Philistia; A Message for Philistia
\p
\v 15 The master¦344712 Yahweh¦344712 says¦344711 this, ‘The Philistines¦344716 have \add ≈chosen\add* hateful vengeance and¦344718 \add ≈have put much effort into trying\add* to destroy¦344722 \add *Yehudah\add*.\x + \xo 25:15-17: \xt Yoel 3:4-8; Amos 1:6-8; Jdg 13:1; 1Sam 4:1.\x*
\v 16 So this is what the master¦344729 Yahweh¦344729 says¦344728: Listen, I'll \add ≈take action\add* against the Philistines¦344736, and I'll cut¦344737 off the Kerethites¦344740 and destroy¦344741 the remnant¦344744 along the coast¦344745.
\v 17 I'll¦344748 take severe vengeance against them with¦344749 furious acts to punish them—they'll know¦344754 that I am Yahweh¦344758, when I take my vengeance on¦344749 them.’ ”
\c 26
\s1 Tsor's punishment
\rem /s1 Prophecy against Tyre; A Prophecy Against Tyre; Proclamation against Tyre; A Message for Tyre
\p
\v 1 \add ≈At the beginning\add* of the month¦344772 in¦344767 the eleventh year¦344770 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*, Yahweh¦344776 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦344774:\x + \xo 26:1–28:19: \xt Yoel 3:4-8; Amos 1:9-10.\x*
\v 2 “Humanity's child, Tsor \add (Tyre¦344787)\add* has said¦344786 against Yerushalem, ‘\add ≈Nice one\add*! \add ≈Yerushalem's¦344790\add* gates are broken¦344792. \add ≈It's going my way—I can take advantage\add* because¦344783 it's been destroyed.’
\p
\v 3 Because of that, the master¦344803 Yahweh¦344803 says¦344802 this: ‘Listen, I'm opposed to you, Tsor, and I'll¦344808 raise up many nations¦344810 against you, just like the sea¦344813 raises its¦344814 waves¦344814.
\v 4 They'll destroy¦344816 Tsor's walls¦344817 and tear down its towers¦344820, then¦344816 I'll¦344821 sweep its dust away and make¦344824 it like a bare rock¦344827.
\v 5 It'll become a place¦344829 in the middle¦344832 of the sea¦344833 for \add people to\add* spread¦344829 their nets¦344830 \add out to dry them\add*, since I've¦344836 declared it, and it'll become plunder¦344841 for the nations¦344842. That's the master¦344838 Yahweh's¦344839 declaration.
\v 6 Its \add ≈young women¦344844\add* who are in¦344847 the countryside¦344846 will be slaughtered by swords¦344847, and¦344849 then¦344844 they'll know¦344849 that¦344845 I am Yahweh¦344853.’
\p
\v 7 \add ≈Yes,\add* this is what Yahweh¦344859 says: Listen, I'm bringing¦344862 the Babylonian King¦344867 Nebuchadnezzar¦344866, king of kings¦344872, from the north¦344870, against¦344863 Tsor, with horses¦344873 and chariots¦344874, and with horsemen¦344875 and a \add ≈large army\add*.
\v 8 He'll kill¦344884 your daughters in¦344883 the countryside¦344882. He'll set up a siege¦344887 program and build¦344888 a ramp against your walls and lift¦344891 up shields¦344893 against you.
\v 9 He'll use his battering¦344896 rams to \add ≈smash down\add* your walls¦344898, and¦344895 he'll tear down your towers¦344899 with¦344898 his axes¦344901.
\v 10 \add ≈They'll have\add* so many horses¦344904 that their dust will cover¦344905 you¦344905. Your walls will shake¦344911 with¦344913 the noise¦344907 of \add their\add* horsemen, wagons¦344909, and chariots¦344910. When he \add ≈rides through\add* your gates¦344914, he'll \add pick his way over the stones\add* like men do when entering¦344913 a city¦344916 whose walls have been broken¦344917 down.
\v 11 The hooves¦344919 of his horses¦344920 will trample¦344921 all of your streets¦344926. He'll slaughter your people¦344927 with¦344928 the sword¦344928 and your mighty stone pillars¦344930 will \add ≈be knocked\add* to the ground¦344932.
\v 12 They'll tear down your walls¦344940 and destroy your nice homes—plundering¦344935 your riches and looting your merchandise¦344938. Then¦344935 they'll throw your \add building\add* stones¦344944, wood, and rubble into the \add ≈ocean\add*.
\v 13 I'll¦344951 stop the sound¦344954 of your songs¦344953, and¦344951 the \add ≈music\add* from your harps¦344955 won't¦344956 be heard¦344957 anymore.
\v 14 I'll¦344960 make you \add back into\add* a bare rock—you'll become a place¦344963 where nets are spread¦344963 \add out to dry\add*. You'll never be built¦344967 again¦344968, because¦344969 I, master Yahweh, have spoken¦344972. That's the master Yahweh's¦344971 declaration.
\rem /s1 The Effect of Tyre's Destruction
\p
\v 15 The master¦344980 Yahweh¦344980 says¦344979 this to Tsor, ‘Won't the islands¦344993 shake with the sound¦344985 of your¦344986 downfall¦344986, and with the groans¦344987 of the wounded¦344988 when the terrible slaughter¦344990 is happening in¦344987 you¦344991?
\v 16 Then¦344995 all the \add ≈leaders of the coastal kingdoms\add* will step down from their thrones¦344997 and remove¦345001 their robes¦345004 and take off their embroidered¦345008 clothes. \add Instead,\add* they'll clothe themselves in fear, they'll sit¦345016 on the ground and tremble¦345010 every moment¦345018, and they'll be appalled¦345019 because of you¦345020.
\v 17 Then¦345022 they'll \add ≈sing this song of mourning\add* for you and¦345022 say to you,
\q1 How you, who were inhabited¦345029 \add ≈by sailors\add*, have been destroyed.
\q1 The famous city that¦345033 was so strong, it's now gone from the sea.
\q1 The ones living in¦345036 her¦345038 once spread terror¦345042 around themselves on¦345036 everyone who lived \add ≈nearby\add*.
\q1
\v 18 Now the coastal¦345049 \add kingdoms\add* tremble¦345048 on¦345056 the day¦345050 of your downfall¦345051.
\q1 The islands¦345053 in¦345056 the sea¦345056 are terrified¦345052 by your passing¦345057.’
\p
\v 19 \add ≈Yes,\add* the master¦345063 Yahweh¦345063 says¦345062 this: When I make¦345065 you a desolate¦345068 city¦345067, like the other cities¦345069 that¦345070 are uninhabited¦345073, when I raise up the deep seas against you, and when the great¦345081 waters¦345080 cover¦345079 you,
\v 20 then¦345083 I'll take you down to the pit that ancient people¦345090 went down to, because I'll make you live in the lowest¦345094 realms of the earth like ancient ruins¦345095. Because of that, you won't¦345102 come back and¦345083 \add ≈be splendid¦345105\add* in the land of the living¦345107.
\v 21 I'll¦345110 send disaster onto you, and you won't¦345113 exist anymore. Then \add people\add* will look for you, but¦345111 you'll never be found¦345115 again¦345116. That's the master¦345119 Yahweh's¦345120 declaration.”\x + \xo 26:21: \xt Rev 18:21.\x*
\c 27
\s1 The end of the city of Tsor
\rem /s1 The End of Tyre's Glory; A Lament for Tyre; Lamentation over Tyre; A Funeral Song for Tyre
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦345126 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦345124:
\v 2 “Now you, humanity's¦345133 child, begin a \add ≈funeral song\add* about Tsor \add (Tyre¦345137)\add*,
\v 3 and¦345140 tell Tsor (\add ≈which¦345142 is on a coastal island\add*—merchants to people¦345149 groups on many \add other\add* islands) that¦345142 the master¦345156 Yahweh¦345156 says¦345155 this to you:
\q1 Tsor, you've¦345160 said¦345160, ‘\add ≈My beauty¦345163 has reached perfection¦345162\add*.’
\q1
\v 4 Your borders¦345167 are \add ≈surrounded by the ocean\add*.
\q1 Your builders¦345168 have perfected¦345169 your beauty¦345170.\f + \fr 27:4 \ft Here, the island city of Tyre is imagined as a ship in the ocean.\f*
\q1
\v 5 They made all your planks¦345179 with cypress from \add Mt. Hermon\add* (Senir¦345173).
\q1 They got a cedar¦345180 from Lebanon¦345181 to make a mast for you¦345185.
\q1
\v 6 They made your oars from the oak trees in Bashan¦345188.
\q1 They made your decks out of cypress wood from \add ≈Cyprus¦345199\add*, and overlaid them with ivory¦345194.
\q1
\v 7 Your sails were made from colourful linen¦345202 from Egypt¦345205 \add (Heb. Mitsrayim)\add* that served as your banner¦345210.
\q1 The colours of blue and purple¦345212 from the coasts¦345213 of Elishah are used for the awning¦345216 on¦345204 your boat.
\q1
\v 8 Those who were living in¦345227 Tsidon and Arvad¦345220 were your rowers.
\q1 The wise men of Tsor were within you¦345227—they were your pilots¦345229.
\q1
\v 9 \add ≈Experienced craftsmen\add* from Geval \add ≈sealed\add* your seams¦345237.
\q1 All the ships¦345240 and their¦345242 sailors¦345242 among you were carrying your merchandise¦345246 for trade¦345245.
\q1
\v 10 \add ≈Warriors from\add* Persia, Lydia, and Libya were in¦345252 your army.
\q1 They hung their shields¦345255 and helmets¦345256 within you¦345259.
\q1 They displayed your splendour¦345262.
\q1
\v 11 The men of Arvad¦345265 and Helek in your army were on your walls surrounding you,
\q1 and the people¦345264 from Gammad were in your towers¦345272.
\q1 They hung up their¦345274 shields¦345274 on your walls all around you.
\q1 They completed your beauty¦345282.
\p
\v 12 “Tarshish¦345284 was a trading partner with¦345290 you because¦345286 of your abundant¦345286 wealth¦345289 of goods. They traded¦345285 silver¦345290, iron, tin, and lead¦345293 for your wares¦345295.
\v 13 Yavan, Tubal¦345298, and Meshech¦345299 traded¦345301 \add ≈slaves\add*, as well as bronze¦345305 items, for your¦345307 merchandise¦345307.
\v 14 Beyt-Togarmah¦345310 provided horses¦345311, stallions, and mules¦345313 in exchange¦345314 for your¦345315 products.
\v 15 People from Dedan¦345318 \add (Rhodes Island)\add* were your¦345319 traders¦345319 on many coasts. \add ≈You sent\add* the merchandise¦345322 \add and\add* they¦345328 sent back ivory¦345325 tusks and ebony¦345326 \add wood\add* as payment¦345329.
\v 16 Syria \add (Heb. Aram)\add* was a dealer in¦345335 your many products¦345334—they¦345341 provided emeralds, purple¦345336 dye, coloured cloth, fine fabric, pearls, and rubies¦345340 for your merchandise¦345342.
\v 17 Yehudah and Yisrael¦345346 were trading with¦345349 you¦345348. They provided wheat¦345349 from Minnit¦345350, cakes, honey¦345352, oil¦345353, and balsam for your¦345356 merchandise¦345356.
\v 18 Damascus¦345358 was a trader¦345359 of all your products¦345361, of all your enormous wealth¦345365, and of the wine from Helbon¦345367 and the wool from Zahar.\f + \fr 27:18 \ft Possibly, Zahar (used only once in the Hebrew scriptures) could refer to the white colour of the wool.\f*
\v 19 Dan and Yavan¦345372 from Uzal¦345373 \add ≈provided you with¦345374\add* merchandise of wrought¦345377 iron, cinnamon, and \add fragrant\add* calamus¦345379 \add seeds\add* which became your¦345380 merchandise.
\v 20 Dedan was your¦345384 dealer in¦345385 woven saddle blankets.
\v 21 Arabia¦345390 and all of Kedar's¦345394 leaders were traders¦345396 with you—they¦345395 provided you with lambs¦345398, rams, and goats¦345400.
\v 22 Traders from Sheba¦345405 and Ramah came to sell you¦345408 the best of every¦345410 spice¦345412 and all kinds of precious¦345416 gems—they traded¦345404 gold¦345417 for your¦345408 goods.
\v 23 Haran, Kanneh, and Eden¦345423 were traders¦345424 with you¦345428, along with Sheba¦345425, Ashur, and Kilmad.
\v 24 Those were your traders¦345431 in fancy robes made¦345440 of violet cloth with¦345432 woven colours, and in blankets¦345436 of multicoloured¦345435, embroidered¦345435, and well-woven cloth \add ≈in your marketplaces¦345441\add*.
\v 25 Tarshish's¦345444 ships¦345443 were the transporters of your¦345446 merchandise¦345446.
\q1 So¦345444 you¦345445 were filled¦345447 up—heavily¦345448 laden with¦345450 cargo \add there\add* in¦345450 the heart¦345450 of the seas.
\rem /s1 The Destruction of Tyre
\q1
\v 26 Your¦345459 rowers¦345457 have brought¦345456 you into vast seas.
\q1 The east wind¦345459 has wrecked¦345461 you in¦345462 the middle of them.
\q1
\v 27 Your¦345486 wealth, merchandise, and trade goods,
\q1 your sailors¦345469, pilots¦345469, and ship-builders,
\q1 your traders of merchandise and all the men¦345477 of war¦345478 who are in¦345486 you¦345486, and all your crew—
\q1 they¦345487 will sink into the depths of the sea \add ≈at¦345486 the time\add* of your destruction.
\q1
\v 28 The surroundings will shake
\q1 when they¦345496 hear your¦345495 pilot's¦345495 cry \add of distress\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 29 All \add ≈the oarsmen\add* will come¦345499 down off their¦345500 ships¦345500.
\q1 Mariners and¦345499 all the pilots on the sea¦345508 will stand¦345512 on the land,
\q1
\v 30 then¦345514 they'll raise¦345514 their voices¦345516 towards you¦345515 and¦345514 will wail bitterly¦345518.
\q1 They'll throw¦345519 dust up onto their heads¦345523. They'll roll around \add to get covered\add* in¦345516 ashes¦345524.
\q1
\v 31 They'll shave¦345527 their heads bald for you and \add ≈dress in¦345534\add* \add rough\add* sackcloth,\f + \fr 27:30 \ft All of these (dust, ashes, shaving, sackcloth) are traditional signs of mourning.\f*
\q1 and they'll weep bitterly¦345534 over you and they will cry out \add with¦345534 loud moans\add*.
\q1
\v 32 They will wail \add ≈loudly\add* for you and¦345540 sing \add ≈funeral songs\add* about you.
\q1 Who is like \add the city of\add* Tsor, which has now been brought to silence¦345548 in¦345542 the middle¦345549 of the sea¦345550?
\q1
\v 33 When your merchandise went¦345552 ashore from the sea, it pleased many people¦345556 groups.
\q1 You enriched kings¦345562 \add ≈across the world\add* with¦345558 your great wealth¦345559 and merchandise.
\q1
\v 34 But¦345573 when you¦345576 were shattered by the seas, by deep waters¦345571,
\q1 your merchandise¦345572 and all your crew sank.
\q1
\v 35 All the inhabitants¦345580 of the coastal¦345581 \add kingdoms\add* were appalled¦345582 at you¦345583,
\q1 and their kings¦345584 shuddered¦345585 in horror—their faces¦345588 trembled.
\q1
\v 36 The merchants of the people¦345591 hiss at¦345591 you.
\q1 You've become¦345595 a horror, and you \add ≈won't¦345596 exist anymore\add*.”
\c 28
\s1 Against the pride of Tsor's king
\rem /s1 Prophecy against the King of Tyre; A Message for Tyre's King; A Prophecy Against the King of Tyre; Proclamation against the King of Tyre
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦345605 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦345603:
\v 2 “Humanity's child, tell Tsor's ruler¦345613 that the master¦345619 Yahweh¦345619 says this: Your heart is proud and you've said, “I'm a god. I'll sit in the seat of gods¦345628 in the \add ≈vital centre\add* of the oceans.” Even though you're a man and not a god, you \add ≈tell yourself that you're\add* a god.
\p
\v 3 Listen, \add you king of Tsor\add*, are you wiser than Daniyel was? Are there any secrets¦345650 that¦345650 you don't¦345651 \add already\add* know?
\v 4 You've used your wisdom¦345654 and skill to make yourself¦345657 wealthy¦345658, and amassed¦345659 gold¦345660 and silver¦345661 in¦345655 your treasuries¦345662.
\v 5 By great¦345664 wisdom¦345665 and by your trading¦345666, you've multiplied your wealth, \add ≈but¦345669\add* \add now\add* \add ≈you've become¦345669\add* arrogant because of your wealth.
\p
\v 6 Therefore, the master¦345677 Yahweh¦345677 says¦345676 this: Because you've¦345680 \add ≈convinced yourself that you're invincible like\add* a god¦345685,
\v 7 I'll bring foreigners against you—terrifying \add ≈warriors\add* from other nations¦345693. They'll bring their¦345695 swords¦345695 against the beauty¦345698 of your wisdom¦345699, and they'll defile¦345700 your splendour¦345701.
\v 8 They'll send you down to the pit, and you'll die \add violently\add* like those who die at¦345708 sea.
\v 9 Will you really say, “I'm a god” to the face¦345715 of one who's \add about to\add* kill you? You're a man and not God¦345713, and you'll be \add ≈at¦345722 the mercy\add* of the one who \add ≈runs you through\add*.
\v 10 You'll die¦345727 \add ≈like an ungodly person killed by\add* foreigners, because¦345731 I've¦345733 declared it. That's the master¦345735 Yahweh's¦345736 declaration.’ ”
\s1 The fall of the king of Tsor
\rem /s1 The Fall of the King of Tyre; Lamentation over the King of Tyre
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh¦345742 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦345740:
\v 12 “Humanity's child, \add ≈sing a mourning song\add* for the king¦345753 of Tsor \add (Tyre¦345754)\add* and tell him that the master¦345759 Yahweh¦345759 says¦345758 this:
\q1 You were the model of perfection,
\q1 full of wisdom¦345765 and perfect in beauty¦345767.
\q1
\v 13 You were in Eden—God's garden¦345770.
\q1 Every precious¦345777 stone¦345776 covered¦345778 you:
\q1 ruby, topaz¦345780, emerald¦345787, chrysolite¦345782,
\q1 onyx, jasper¦345784, sapphire¦345785, turquoise¦345786, and beryl¦345787.
\q1 Your settings¦345790 and mountings¦345791 were made from gold¦345788.
\q1 They were prepared¦345795 on the \add very\add* day¦345793 that you were created¦345794.
\q1
\v 14 I placed¦345802 you on¦345803 God's sacred¦345804 mountain¦345803
\q1 like the winged creatures that¦345801 I appointed¦345802 to guard¦345801 humanity.
\q1 You were among the fiery stones¦345808 where you walked¦345811 around.
\q1
\v 15 You had integrity in¦345815 your¦345816 ways¦345815 from the day¦345816 you were created¦345817
\q1 until¦345818 injustice was found¦345820 within you.
\q1
\v 16 Through your¦345827 great trade¦345825 you were filled¦345826 with violence¦345828, and so you sinned¦345829.
\q1 Then I threw you out of God's mountain, like someone who was defiled,
\q1 and I destroyed¦345833 you, guardian¦345835 winged creature,
\q1 and drove you from among¦345836 the fiery stones¦345837.
\q1
\v 17 Your heart¦345842 was arrogant due to your beauty¦345843.
\q1 You ruined your wisdom¦345845 because of your splendour¦345848.
\q1 I threw¦345852 you down to the earth.
\q1 I put you in¦345843 front¦345853 of kings¦345854 so they can see you.
\q1
\v 18 Because of \add ≈all your disobedience\add* and your \add ≈dishonest\add* trade¦345862,
\q1 you've defiled your sacred places,
\q1 \add ≈so\add* I've made¦345871 fire¦345867 come out from you to consume¦345870 you.
\q1 I'll turn you into ashes¦345872 on¦345861 the earth¦345875
\q1 in¦345861 the sight¦345876 of everyone who looks at¦345861 you.
\q1
\v 19 Everyone who knew¦345883 you among¦345884 the people¦345884 groups
\q1 will \add ≈shudder to look at¦345884\add* you \add now\add*—
\q1 they will be horrified, and you \add ≈won't¦345889 exist any longer\add*.’ ”
\s1 The punishment of Tsidon
\rem /s1 Proclamation against Sidon; Prophecy against Sidon; A Prophecy Against Sidon; A Message for Sidon
\p
\v 20 Then Yahweh¦345898 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦345896:\x + \xo 28:20-26: \xt Yoel 3:4-8; 1Ki 11:4-5; 1Ki 16:30-33.\x*
\q1
\v 21 “Humanity's child, \add ≈look towards\add* Tsidon \add (Sidon¦345909)\add* and¦345910 prophesy¦345910 against¦345907 it¦345911.
\v 22 Tell \add them\add* that the master¦345916 Yahweh says¦345915 this: ‘Listen, I'm against¦345919 you, Tsidon, because¦345924 I \add ≈intend to\add* be \add ≈praised by you\add*, so your¦345922 people¦345923 will know¦345923 that I am Yahweh, when I execute¦345928 justice within you and¦345913 demonstrate my holiness¦345931 to you.
\v 23 I'll¦345934 send¦345934 a plague to \add afflict\add* you, and \add there'll be\add* blood¦345938 in¦345936 your streets¦345939, and \add ≈people will fall¦345940 dead all around you\add*. When¦345934 the sword¦345943 comes against you from all around, then¦345934 you'll know¦345946 that I am Yahweh¦345950.
\s1 The blessing for Yisrael
\rem /s1 Israel Will Be Blessed
\q1
\v 24 Then¦345952 there'll no longer be pricking¦345959 briars and¦345952 painful thorns¦345960 for the \add ≈Israeli¦345956 people\add* from all those around¦345963 her who despise¦345964 her, so then¦345952 they'll know¦345966 that¦345959 I am the master¦345969 Yahweh¦345969.’
\rem /s1 Future Blessing for Israel; Restoration for Israel
\p
\v 25 The master¦345976 Yahweh¦345976 says that when I gather¦345978 the \add ≈Jewish people¦345986\add* from the \add ≈nations\add* that they were scattered¦345988 among, and when they \add ≈properly honour\add* me so the nations may see, then they'll make their¦345997 homes in¦345978 the land¦345997 I gave to my servant¦346000 Yakov \add (Jacob)\add*.
\v 26 Then¦346003 they'll live securely within \add @the land\add* and build¦346006 houses¦346007, plant¦346008 vineyards¦346009, and live securely. When¦346003 I execute¦346012 justice on¦346012 all the people all around¦346017 who now despise¦346015 them, then¦346003 they'll know¦346018 that I am their¦346022 god¦346022 Yahweh¦346021.’ ”
\c 29
\s1 The punishment of Egypt
\rem /s1 Prophecy against Egypt; A Message for Egypt; Proclamation against Egypt; A Prophecy Against Egypt
\p
\v 1 \add ≈At the beginning of January\add* in¦346025 the twelfth year¦346025 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*, Yahweh¦346034 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦346032:\x + \xo 29:1–32:32: \xt Isa 19:1-25; Jer 46:2-26.\x*
\v 2 “Humanity's child, \add ≈stand firmly\add* against Egypt's¦346047 king¦346046 Far-oh \add (Pharaoh¦346045)\add*. Prophesy against him and against all of Egypt,
\v 3 telling \add them\add* that the master¦346061 Yahweh¦346061 says this: Listen, I'm against¦346064 you, Far-oh, king¦346066 of Egypt¦346068—you, the great¦346070 sea¦346069 monster¦346069 that lurks in the middle¦346072 of the river, who says, “My river is my own. I've made¦346079 it for myself¦346079.”
\v 4 I'll put hooks¦346082 into your jaw, and¦346081 the fish from your streams will cling to the scales on¦346084 your \add side\add*. I'll lift you up from the middle of your river, along with¦346084 all the river's fish that cling to your scales.
\v 5 I'll fling¦346101 you into the wilderness¦346102—you and all the fish¦346107 from your river. You'll fall¦346113 on the open countryside¦346112—\add @your corpse\add* won't¦346114 be taken or moved, \add but¦346101 rather\add* I'll give¦346122 you as food to the birds and wild animals¦346118.
\v 6 Then¦346125 all of Egypt's¦346129 inhabitants¦346128 will know¦346125 that I¦346131 am Yahweh¦346132, because¦346133 they've been \add like a brittle\add* reed to the \add ≈kingdom\add* of Yisrael¦346138.\x + \xo 29:6: \xt Isa 36:6.\x*
\v 7 When \add @Yisrael\add* \add ≈grabbed you\add*, you broke and tore open their shoulder¦346149, and when they leaned¦346150 on¦346141 you, you broke and made their legs unsteady¦346153.
\v 8 Therefore the master¦346163 Yahweh¦346163 says¦346162 this: Listen, I'll¦346169 send \add ≈an army\add* against you that will cut¦346169 short both people's and animals¦346172' \add lives\add*,
\v 9 and¦346174 Egypt¦346177 will become¦346174 a desolate¦346178 ruin¦346179. Then¦346174 they'll know¦346180 that I am Yahweh¦346184.
\p Because the sea monster said¦346186, “The river is mine, because¦346185 I made¦346190 it,”
\v 10 listen: I'm against you and against your¦346197 river, so I'll¦346198 give Egypt¦346202 over to desolation¦346205 and waste, and you'll become a wasteland from the Migdol¦346206 to Seveneh and the borders¦346210 of Kush.
\v 11 No person or domestic animal¦346220 will pass through it, and it won't¦346221 be inhabited for forty¦346227 years.
\v 12 I'll make Egypt¦346234 a desolation in the middle of uninhabited lands, and its cities will become a desolation in the middle of wasted¦346243 cities for forty¦346246 years, then¦346230 I'll scatter¦346248 \add ≈the Egyptian people\add* among the nations¦346252, and I'll disperse¦346253 them through the lands.
\p
\v 13 \add ≈But\add* the master¦346260 Yahweh¦346260 says¦346259 this: At the end of forty¦346263 years I'll¦346265 gather¦346265 Egypt¦346268 from the people¦346271 groups they¦346274 were scattered¦346274 among,
\v 14 and I'll¦346281 restore¦346277 Egypt's¦346281 fortunes and bring¦346282 them back to the region¦346284 of Patros¦346285, to their¦346289 native¦346289 land. Then¦346277 they'll become an insignificant¦346293 kingdom¦346292 there¦346291.
\v 15 It'll be the least significant kingdom¦346297, and it won't¦346300 \add ≈become prominent\add* again¦346303 among the nations. I'll¦346307 diminish them so that they'll no longer \add ≈dominate\add* \add other\add* nations.
\v 16 \add Requesting help from\add* \add @Egypt\add* will no longer be a reason for the \add ≈kingdom\add* of Yisrael¦346317 to be confident¦346318, instead, \add @Egypt\add* will be a reminder of Yisrael's \add ≈disobedience\add* when they turned¦346321 to \add @Egypt\add* for help. Then¦346312 they'll know¦346323 that¦346319 I am the master¦346326 Yahweh¦346326.”
\s1 Nebuchadnetstsar's defeat of Egypt
\rem /s1 King Nebuchadnezzar Will Conquer Egypt; Nebuchadnezzar to Conquer Egypt; Babylonia Will Plunder Egypt
\p
\v 17 \add ≈At the beginning of May\add* in¦346331 the 27th year¦346333 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*,\f + \fr 29:17 \ft 571 B.C.\f* Yahweh¦346340 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦346338:
\v 18 “Humanity's child, the Babylonian King¦346348 Nebuchadnetstsar stationed his army to do hard work against \add the fortified island city of\add* Tsor \add (Tyre¦346359)\add*. Every head¦346362 was rubbed until it was made bald, and every¦346360 shoulder¦346366 was made raw. Yet he and his army received no payment from Tsor for the hard work that¦346378 he carried out against it,
\v 19 therefore the master¦346387 Yahweh¦346387 says¦346386 this, ‘Listen, I'm giving Egypt¦346398 to the Babylonian King¦346392 Nebuchadnetstsar and he'll take¦346403 away its wealth¦346400, plunder¦346404 its possessions, and carry¦346399 off everything he finds there—that will be his army's wages.
\v 20 I've¦346414 given¦346414 him the land¦346418 of Egypt¦346419 as the wages for the work they¦346421 did for me. That's the master¦346424 Yahweh's¦346425 declaration.
\p
\v 21 On that day¦346428 I'll make¦346430 \add ≈power\add* sprout up for the \add ≈kingdom\add* of Yisrael¦346433, and I'll \add ≈put you¦346434 among¦346439 them to speak out\add* so they'll know¦346440 that I am Yahweh¦346444.’ ”
\c 30
\s1 Yahweh's punishment of Egypt
\rem /s1 A Lament for Egypt; Lamentation for Egypt; The Lord Will Punish Egypt; A Sad Day for Egypt
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦346450 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦346448:
\q1
\v 2 “Humanity's child, prophesy¦346457 and¦346458 say¦346458 that the master¦346461 Yahweh¦346461 says¦346460 this:
\q1 ‘Wail \add now\add* \add ≈because the coming days won't go well\add*.
\q1
\v 3 The day is near—the day is near for Yahweh¦346473.
\q1 It'll be a day of clouds¦346475—a time of doom for nations¦346477.
\q1
\v 4 Then¦346480 \add ≈an army\add* will come¦346480 against Egypt¦346482,
\q1 and there'll be anguish¦346484 in Kush when the people¦346489 are killed in Egypt—
\q1 when they take¦346489 its wealth¦346490,
\q1 and when its foundations¦346492 get torn apart.
\p
\v 5 Kush and Put¦346495, Lydia, all Arabia¦346499, and Libya¦346500, together with¦346505 the people belonging to the agreement¦346503—they'll all be killed by the sword¦346505.
\v 6 Yahweh says¦346510 this: The ones who support¦346513 Egypt \add militarily\add* will fall, and the pride¦346516 of \add @Egypt's\add* strength¦346517 will \add ≈collapse\add*. From Migdol¦346518 to Seveneh \add @their combined warriors\add* will be killed by the sword¦346520. That's the master¦346525 Yahweh's¦346511 declaration.
\v 7 \add @Those allies\add* will be appalled there among the abandoned lands¦346530, and¦346528 their¦346532 cities will be among all the \add other\add* ruined cities.
\v 8 Then¦346539 they'll know¦346539 that I am Yahweh¦346543, when I set fire¦346546 to Egypt¦346547, and¦346548 when all its¦346551 helpers¦346551 are destroyed.
\p
\v 9 \add ≈At that time\add*, messengers¦346556 will go out from in front¦346557 of me in ships¦346558 to terrorise Kush \add ≈when they think they're secure¦346563\add*, and there'll¦346570 be anguish¦346565 among them on¦346558 the day of Egypt's¦346568 doom. \add ≈Yes, take notice,\add* it's coming¦346571.
\v 10 The master¦346576 Yahweh¦346576 says¦346575 this: I'll¦346578 \add ≈use\add* the Babylonian King¦346585 Nebuchadnetstsar to bring Egypt's¦346582 multitudes¦346581 to an end¦346578.
\v 11 He and his army with him, the terror of nations¦346593, will be brought¦346594 in to destroy¦346595 the land. They'll draw out their¦346598 swords¦346598 against Egypt¦346601 and fill¦346602 the land with those they kill¦346595.
\v 12 I'll make the rivers into dry ground¦346610, and I'll sell the land into the hands of wicked¦346617 men. I'll make the land and its¦346620 abundant \add produce\add* desolate¦346618 by the actions of strangers¦346623. I, Yahweh¦346625, have spoken¦346626.
\p
\v 13 The master¦346632 Yahweh¦346632 says this: I'll destroy¦346634 idols, and I'll bring an end to the worthless¦346637 idols in Memphis¦346638 \add (Heb. ‘Nof’¦346638)\add*. There won't¦346643 be a prince¦346639 in the land of Egypt any longer, and I'll put terror into Egypt.
\v 14 Then¦346652 I'll make \add the\add* Patros¦346655 \add region\add* desolate and set fire¦346657 to Tsoan¦346658 \add city\add*, and I'll execute¦346659 judgement¦346660 against Thebes¦346661 \add (Heb. ‘No¦346661’, the capital of southern Egypt)\add*.
\v 15 I'll¦346663 pour¦346663 out my fury on Egypt's¦346669 stronghold¦346668 Pelusium \add (Heb. ‘Sin¦346667’)\add*, and¦346670 cut¦346670 off the crowd¦346673 at Thebes¦346674.
\v 16 Then¦346676 I'll¦346676 set fire¦346677 to Egypt¦346678: Pelusium¦346682 will be in¦346678 great agony¦346679, Thebes¦346683 will be broken¦346685 up, and¦346676 Memphis¦346686 will face enemies every day.
\v 17 The young¦346690 men¦346690 of Aven and Pi-Veset \add cities\add* will be killed by the sword¦346695, and their \add ≈populations will be taken\add* into captivity¦346698.
\v 18 In \add Egypt's\add* Tahfanhes, the day¦346703 will hold back its light when I break¦346704 Egypt's \add ≈power and control\add* there¦346706, and its pride¦346714 in¦346701 its strength¦346715 will be finished. There'll be a cloud¦346717 covering¦346718 it, and its daughters¦346719 will \add ≈be taken away\add* into captivity¦346720.
\v 19 I'll¦346723 execute¦346723 acts of judgement¦346724 in¦346725 Egypt¦346725, then¦346723 they'll know¦346726 that I am Yahweh¦346730.’ ”
\s1 Yahweh breaks Egypt's power
\rem /s1 The Broken Arms of Pharaoh; The Broken Power of the King of Egypt; Proclamation against Pharaoh
\p
\v 20 \add ≈At the beginning of May\add* in the eleventh year¦346736 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*,\f + \fr 30:20 \ft (Back in time to) 587 B.C.\f* Yahweh¦346743 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦346741:
\v 21 “Humanity's child, I've¦346757 broken¦346757 the Egyptian king¦346754 Far-oh's arm. Listen, it hasn't been \add ≈put in¦346769 a splint\add*, or set to heal with¦346769 a bandage¦346765, so that it'll become strong¦346767 enough \add again\add* to hold a sword¦346769.
\v 22 Therefore the master¦346777 Yahweh¦346777 says¦346775 this, ‘Listen, I'm against¦346780 the Egyptian king¦346783 Far-oh and I'll break¦346786 both his strong¦346792 arm and the \add previously\add* broken¦346795 one, and I'll make¦346796 the sword fall¦346796 from his \add ≈grip\add*.
\v 23 Then¦346802 I'll¦346807 scatter¦346802 Egypt¦346805 among the \add other\add* nations¦346806 and¦346807 disperse¦346807 \add @their people\add* among the lands¦346808.
\v 24 I'll¦346810 strengthen¦346810 the Babylonian king's arms and¦346810 place my sword¦346819 in his hand¦346820 so that I can destroy Far-oh's arms. He'll groan in front of the Babylonian king with the groans of a dying man.
\v 25 I'll¦346831 strengthen¦346831 the arms of the Babylonian king, while Far-oh's arms will fall. Then¦346831 they'll know that I am Yahweh¦346844, when I put my sword¦346846 into the hand¦346847 of the king of Babylon¦346836 and¦346831 he \add ≈uses\add* it against¦346853 Egypt¦346856.
\v 26 I'll¦346858 scatter¦346858 Egypt¦346861 among the \add other\add* nations¦346862 and¦346858 disperse¦346863 \add @their people\add* through the lands¦346865. Then¦346858 they'll know¦346866 that I am Yahweh¦346870.”
\c 31
\s1 Egypt gets compared to fallen Assyria
\rem /s1 Egypt Is Compared to a Cedar Tree; A Cedar in Lebanon; Egypt Compared to Fallen Assyria; The Lofty Cedar
\p
\v 1 \add ≈At the end of June\add* in¦346874 the eleventh year¦346876 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*,\f + \fr 31:1 \ft 587 B.C.\f* Yahweh¦346883 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦346881:
\v 2 “Humanity's child, \add ≈ask\add* the Egyptian king¦346894 Far-oh \add (Pharaoh¦346893)\add* and his multitudes¦346899 around him, ‘\add ≈Who else is as powerful as you¦346903 and your¦346904 people¦346899\add*?
\v 3 Listen, Assyria¦346907 was \add like\add* a cedar¦346908 in¦346909 Lebanon¦346909 with¦346909 beautiful¦346910 branches, giving shade¦346913 to the forest¦346912—extremely tall, and the branches formed its treetop¦346919.
\v 4 \add ≈A deep water supply\add* made¦346922 it grow tall. Rivers flowed all around¦346929 its area, because their channels¦346933 stretched out to all the trees¦346938 in the countryside¦346939.
\v 5 It \add ≈grew taller\add* than any of the other trees¦346947 in the countryside¦346948, and its branches¦346952 grew long¦346951 and \add ≈thick\add* because¦346954 of \add ≈having plenty of\add* water¦346954 in its shoots¦346956.
\v 6 Every \add ≈kind of\add* bird¦346962 nested in its branches¦346965, while every living thing in the countryside¦346969 gave¦346966 birth¦346966 to its young under the foliage of that tree. All the powerful nations¦346973 lived under its shade¦346970.
\v 7 It was beautiful¦346976 in¦346977 its greatness¦346977 and¦346976 due to the length¦346978 of its branches¦346979, because¦346980 its roots¦346983 \add ≈had access to\add* many water¦346986 \add sources\add*.
\v 8 Cedar¦346989 trees in God's garden couldn't equal it. None of the cypress trees matched its branches, nor could its branches be equalled by the plane¦347002 tree. There was no other tree in God's garden that could compare to its beauty¦347017.\x + \xo 31:8: \xt Gen 2:9.\x*
\v 9 I made¦347020 it beautiful with¦347021 its many branches¦347022, and all the trees¦347026 in God's garden¦347030 in Eden¦347028 envied¦347023 it.
\p
\v 10 Therefore the master¦347037 Yahweh¦347037 says¦347036 this: Because it was so tall, and it set its treetop¦347044 between¦347047 its branches¦347048, and \add ≡because\add* it \add ≈grew proud¦347049\add* of its height¦347051,
\v 11 I \add ≈handed it over to\add* a powerful nation¦347056 to deal with¦347054 it according¦347060 to what its wickedness¦347060 deserves. I've thrown¦347061 it out,
\v 12 and foreigners who were the terror of all the nations cut it off and left it to die. Its branches¦347075 fell onto the mountains¦347070 and all the valleys¦347073, and lay broken¦347076 in¦347078 all the land's ravines¦347079. Then¦347063 all the nations on¦347078 earth came out from under its shade¦347082 and went away from it.
\v 13 All the \add ≈various kinds of\add* birds rested on the trunk¦347091 of the fallen¦347091 tree, and every \add kind of\add* animal¦347102 in the countryside¦347103 came to its branches¦347099.
\v 14 That happened so that no other trees that grow by the waters will raise up their¦347129 foliage to the height of the tallest trees, and that no other trees that grow beside the waters will reach up to that height. They've all been assigned to death¦347139, to the earth¦347142 below, among humanity's¦347146 children¦347145, with¦347110 those¦347149 that go down into the pit.
\p
\v 15 The master¦347156 Yahweh¦347156 says this: On the day¦347158 when the cedar went down to \add ≈the place of the dead\add*, I brought mourning to the earth. I covered¦347162 the deep waters over it, and I held back the oceans. I held back the great waters, and I brought mourning to Lebanon¦347174 for it. So all the trees¦347177 in the countryside¦347178 mourned because of it.
\v 16 I brought¦347186 shuddering to the nations¦347185 at¦347186 the sound¦347182 of its downfall¦347183, when I threw it down to \add ≈the place of the dead\add*, along with¦347186 those¦347191 who went down into the pit. So I comforted¦347193 all the trees from Eden¦347200 in¦347186 the lowest¦347195 parts of the earth. Those had been the choicest¦347201 and best trees from Lebanon¦347204—the trees that drank the waters¦347208.
\v 17 They also went¦347214 down with¦347223 \add @the big tree\add* to \add ≈the place of the dead\add*, to the ones who'd been killed by the sword¦347220. Those were its strong arm—those¦347218 nations¦347225 who'd lived in¦347223 its shade¦347223.
\p
\v 18 \add Egypt,\add* which of the trees in Eden was your equal in glory¦347232 and greatness¦347233? You'll be brought¦347237 down with Eden's¦347236 trees to the lowest¦347247 parts of the earth¦347246 among¦347234 the uncircumcised¦347249. You'll live there with those¦347253 who were killed by the sword¦347255.’
\m That's \add the fate of\add* Far-oh \add (Pharaoh¦347257)\add* and all his multitudes¦347260. That's the master¦347263 Yahweh's¦347264 declaration.”
\c 32
\s1 Mourning over Egypt and its king
\rem /s1 A Lament for Pharaoh; A Warning for Pharaoh; The King of Egypt Is Compared to a Crocodile; Lamentation over Pharaoh and Egypt
\p
\v 1 \add ≈At the beginning of March\add* in¦347268 the twelfth year¦347270 \add since King Yoyakin (Jehoiachin) had been taken captive\add*,\f + \fr 32:1 \ft 585 B.C.\f* Yahweh¦347280 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦347278:
\v 2 “Humanity's child, \add ≈sing a loud mourning song\add* about¦347303 the Egyptian King¦347292 Far-oh \add (Pharaoh¦347291)\add* and tell him,
\q1 ‘You're like a young¦347297 lion¦347297 among the nations¦347298,
\q1 but¦347295 you're like the sea monster¦347301 in¦347304 the oceans—
\q1 you churn up the water—
\q1 you stir up the waters¦347307 with¦347304 your feet¦347308, making them muddy.
\q1
\v 3 The¦347315 master¦347314 Yahweh¦347314 says this:
\q1 I'll¦347316 spread¦347316 my net over you in the assembly¦347321 of many people¦347322 groups,
\q1 and they'll haul you up in my net.
\q1
\v 4 I'll abandon¦347327 you in the land.
\q1 I'll throw¦347327 you into the countryside¦347332
\q1 and cause¦347334 \add ≈every kind of\add* bird¦347338 to settle¦347334 on you,
\q1 and animals¦347342 of \add ≈every kind of\add* will gorge¦347340 themselves on you.
\q1
\v 5 I'll¦347347 \add ≈dump\add* your flesh¦347350 on the hills,
\q1 and¦347347 fill¦347354 the valleys¦347355 with your corpse.
\q1
\v 6 Then¦347358 I'll¦347358 drench¦347358 your land with your blood \add across\add* to the hills,
\q1 and¦347358 the ravines¦347365 will be filled¦347366 with your blood.
\q1
\v 7 Then¦347369 when I \add ≈eliminate\add* you, I'll cover the heavens¦347371 and darken¦347372 their¦347375 stars¦347375.
\q1 I'll cover the sun with¦347370 clouds¦347377, and the moon won't¦347380 shine its light.\x + \xo 32:7: \xt Mat 24:29; Rev 6:12-13; 8:12.\x*
\q1
\v 8 I'll darken¦347390 all the shining lights¦347388 in¦347389 the skies¦347389 over you¦347391,
\q1 and I'll put darkness¦347393 over your¦347396 land¦347396. That's the master¦347398 Yahweh's¦347399 declaration.
\p
\v 9 I'll¦347401 disturb¦347401 the hearts¦347402 of many people¦347403 groups in¦347405 lands that¦347411 you don't¦347412 know \add about\add*
\q1 when I bring¦347405 about your¦347406 destruction¦347406 among the nations¦347407—among lands you haven't known¦347414.
\q1
\v 10 I'll shock many people¦347418 groups \add ≈with¦347424 what I'll do to\add* you.
\q1 Their¦347428 kings¦347420 will shudder¦347421 in¦347424 horror concerning you when I swing my sword¦347425 \add ≈towards\add* them.
\q1 On the day¦347433 of your¦347422 downfall¦347434 they'll be trembling¦347429 \add ≈continuously\add* because¦347422 of you.
\p
\v 11 \add ≈Yes,\add* the master¦347440 Yahweh¦347440 says¦347439 this \add to you, Egypt\add*:
\q1 The Babylonian king's sword¦347442 will come¦347446 against you.
\q1
\v 12 I'll¦347450 cause large numbers of you to be killed by warriors' swords¦347448—each warrior a terror of nations¦347453.
\q1 Those warriors will devastate¦347455 the pride¦347458 of Egypt¦347459 and destroy¦347460 all its¦347463 \add ≈many people¦347451\add*.
\q1
\v 13 I'll¦347465 destroy¦347465 all the livestock¦347481 from beside the plentiful waters.
\q1 No human¦347478 foot¦347476 will stir the waters up again¦347479,
\q1 nor will cattle¦347470 hooves¦347480 disturb them.
\q1
\v 14 Then I'll¦347486 calm their waters¦347487 and make their rivers¦347488 run like oil¦347489.
\q1 That's the master¦347492 Yahweh's¦347493 declaration.
\p
\v 15 When I make Egypt's¦347499 territory¦347498 into an abandoned place,
\q1 when the land is \add ≈stripped of all its¦347503 produce\add*,
\q1 when I attack all its inhabitants¦347509, they'll know¦347511 that I am Yahweh¦347515.
\q1
\v 16 There'll be a \add ≈funeral song\add*—the daughters¦347520 of the nations¦347521 will sing mournful songs about it.
\q1 They'll sing sadly about Egypt¦347526—over all its multitudes¦347531 they'll sing dirges.
\q1 That's the master¦347535 Yahweh's¦347536 declaration.’ ”
\s1 Those in the place of the dead
\rem /s1 The World of the Dead; Egypt Falls into the Pit; Dirge over Egypt
\p
\v 17 \add ≈In the middle of that same month¦347545\add*,\f + \fr 32:17 \ft 585 B.C.\f* Yahweh¦347549 \add ≈gave me\add* a message¦347547:
\v 18 “Humanity's child, weep for the \add ≈numerous Egyptians\add* and bring¦347561 them down to the lowest¦347569 parts¦347569 of the earth¦347568. \add ≡Bring\add* \add @Egypt\add* and the daughters¦347563 of majestic nations¦347564 down along with those¦347572 who've \add already\add* gone down into the pit.
\v 19 \add Ask them: Egypt,\add* are you¦347576 really more beautiful than anyone else? Go down and¦347578 lie down with the uncircumcised¦347581.
\p
\v 20 They'll fall among those¦347584 who were killed by the sword. The sword has been drawn¦347590. \add @Egypt\add* has been handed over to the sword—\add @its enemies\add* will seize it and its multitudes¦347594.
\v 21 The strongest of the warriors in \add ≈the place of the dead\add* will declare about Egypt and its allies, ‘They've come¦347606 down here. They'll lie with the uncircumcised¦347608—with those who were killed by the sword¦347611.’
\p
\v 22 Assyria¦347614 is there¦347613 with all its \add ≈people\add*, with its graves¦347619 all around¦347618 it¦347618—all of them were killed by the sword¦347623.
\v 23 Those whose graves are set in¦347628 the pit's recesses\x + \xo 32:23 \xt Isa 14:15.\x* are there, along with¦347628 all its¦347632 \add ≈people\add*. Its¦347632 graves surround all of those who were killed by the sword¦347638—those who brought terror¦347642 onto the land¦347643 of the living¦347644.
\p
\v 24 Eylam is there¦347646 with all its multitudes¦347650, with its graves¦347652 all around¦347651 it—all of them were killed. \add That's the place for\add* those¦347675 who were killed by the sword¦347656, who've gone down uncircumcised¦347660 to the lowest¦347665 parts¦347665 of the earth¦347664, who brought their terrors¦347668 onto the land¦347669 of the living¦347670, and who carry their own shame, together with the ones going down into the pit.
\v 25 They \add ≈prepared\add* a bed¦347681 for Eylam and all its multitudes¦347685 among those who were slain—its graves¦347687 surround it. All of them are uncircumcised¦347689, pierced by the sword¦347692, because¦347693 they brought their terrors¦347696 onto the land¦347697 of the living¦347698. So they carry their own shame, together with the ones going down into the pit among all those who've been killed, those who are going down into the pit. \add @Eylam\add* is among all those who were killed.
\p
\v 26 Meshek¦347710, Tuval, and all their multitudes¦347714 are there¦347709 with their graves¦347716 all around¦347715 them. All of them are uncircumcised¦347718, killed by the sword¦347720, because¦347721 they¦347723 brought their terrors¦347724 onto the land¦347725 of the living¦347726.
\v 27 They¦347738 don't¦347728 lie with the fallen¦347733 warriors of the uncircumcised¦347734 who've gone down to \add ≈the place of the dead\add* with all their weapons¦347739 of war¦347741, and with their swords¦347745 placed under¦347746 their heads¦347747 and their iniquities¦347749 over their bones¦347752, because¦347753 they were the terror¦347755 of warriors in¦347739 the land¦347757 of the living¦347758.
\p
\v 28 \add ≈So¦347760\add* you¦347763, \add Egypt,\add* will be broken¦347763 amongst the uncircumcised¦347762. You'll lie alongside those¦347767 who were pierced by the sword¦347769.
\p
\v 29 Edom¦347772 is \add also\add* there with its kings¦347773 and all its leaders. \add ≈Despite their¦347780 strength¦347780\add*, they've been placed with those killed by the sword¦347785. They lie there along with the uncircumcised¦347789—with those who've gone down into the pit.
\p
\v 30 All the northern¦347798 leaders are there, along with all the Sidonians¦347802 who went down with the ones who'd been pierced. They were powerful and made others afraid, but¦347800 now they're there in¦347809 shame—uncircumcised¦347814 along with those who were pierced by the sword¦347819. They carry their¦347799 own shame, together with the ones going down into the pit.
\p
\v 31 Far-oh \add (Pharaoh¦347829)\add* will look and be comforted¦347830 about all his multitudes¦347835 who were pierced by the sword¦347839—Far-oh and all his army. That's¦347840 the master¦347845 Yahweh's¦347846 declaration.
\p
\v 32 \add ≈Yes,\add* I put him there as my \add ≈agent to terrify those\add* in the land¦347855 of the living¦347856, but¦347857 Far-oh and all his multitudes¦347868 will be laid down among the uncircumcised¦347859—among those pierced by the sword¦347864. That's the master¦347871 Yahweh's¦347872 declaration.”
\c 33
\ms1 The promise of God of his people
\mr (33:1–37:27)
\s1 Yehezkel as Yisrael's watchman
\rem /s1 Ezekiel as Israel's Watchman; God Appoints Ezekiel as a Lookout; The watchman prepares for the restored kingdom; Ezekiel Israel's Sentry; Ezekiel a Watchman
\r (Eze. 3:16-21)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦347878 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦347876:
\v 2 “Humanity's child¦347888, tell this to your people: ‘When I \add ≈send an army\add* against any \add ≈country\add*, then the people of that \add ≈country choose¦347899 one of their¦347905 men to become\add* a watchman¦347909 for them.
\v 3 He looks for \add ≈any attacker coming¦347915 onto\add* the land, and¦347911 then¦347911 he blows¦347919 his horn to warn¦347921 the people¦347924.
\v 4 If the people hear¦347926 the sound¦347930 of the horn but¦347926 don't¦347932 \add ≈take any notice\add*, and if the \add ≈attacker\add* comes¦347934 and kills them, then¦347926 each one's blood¦347937 is on¦347938 their own head¦347938.
\v 5 \add Yes,\add* if someone heard¦347944 the horn \add ≈but paid no attention\add*, \add @that person's\add* blood¦347947 is on them, \add ≈but\add* if \add @those people\add* \add ≈do pay attention\add*, they'll save their own lives.
\v 6 \add ≈However,\add* if the watchman sees the \add ≈attacker\add* coming¦347962, \add ≈but\add* doesn't blow the horn, \add ≈that is, if\add* the people¦347967 weren't warned¦347970, and if the \add ≈attacker approaches\add* and takes anyone's¦347975 life, then¦347955 that person dies in¦347966 their own sin, \add ≈but\add* I'll¦347983 require¦347983 their blood¦347979 from the watchman.’
\p
\v 7 Now, \add ≈talking to\add* you, humanity's¦347989 child: I've¦347991 made you a watchman¦347990 for the \add ≈Israeli¦347993 people\add*. You'll hear¦347994 the words from my mouth¦347995 and then warn¦347997 them on my behalf.
\v 8 If I say to a wicked person, ‘You wicked person, you're certainly about to die,’ and if you don't¦348006 announce it so as to warn that wicked \add person\add* about their behaviour, then that wicked \add person\add* will die in¦348013 their sin, but¦348006 \add ≈it'll be you that I hold responsible for their death\add*.
\v 9 \add ≈But¦348019\add* if you warn the wicked¦348023 person about their behaviour, so that they might turn back from it, and¦348019 if that person doesn't turn back from their behaviour, then¦348019 they'll die in¦348032 their sin, but¦348019 you yourself will have saved your own life¦348035.
\s1 Each person is responsible for their own deeds
\rem /s1 Individual Responsibility; The Watchman's Message; God's Justice and Mercy
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So,\add* humanity's¦348042 child, you¦348049 tell the \add ≈Israeli¦348046 people\add*, ‘You're saying this, “Our errors and our disobedience \add ≈have come back on us and they're hurting\add* us. How can we go on living \add like this\add*?” ’
\v 11 Tell them that this is the master¦348070 Yahweh's¦348071 declaration: ‘As I live, I don't delight in¦348075 the death¦348075 of wicked people. If they turn away from their wicked behaviour, then they'll live! \add ≈So turn back\add*, \add yes\add* turn away from your wicked behaviour. Why should you \add ≈Israeli¦348090 people\add* die¦348089?’
\p
\v 12 \add ≈So now\add*, humanity's¦348097 child¦348101, you tell your people, ‘Righteous people's righteousness won't save¦348107 them if they sin. Wicked people's wickedness¦348111 won't cause them to die if they turn away from their sin. \add ≈No,\add* the righteous person won't be able to live¦348122 because of his righteousness if he sins¦348125.
\v 13 If I say that a righteous person will surely live, and if they trust in¦348145 their righteousness and then commit injustice¦348145, I won't¦348143 call to mind any of \add ≈the good things they did\add*—that person will die¦348150 for the wickedness they committed.
\v 14 \add ≈Again,\add* if I say to the wicked¦348153, “You will surely¦348154 die,” but¦348152 if they then¦348152 turn away from their sins and do¦348158 what's just and right¦348160,
\v 15 \add ≈like\add* returning¦348163 loan guarantees that were wickedly demanded, or \add ≈like\add* making restitution for what they've stolen, and then if they \add ≈obey the instructions¦348167\add* that give life¦348168 and no longer \add ≈disobey\add*, then \add @those formerly wicked people\add* will surely live—they won't¦348170 die¦348176.
\v 16 None of the sins¦348180 that¦348182 they've committed will be called to mind against them. Those who've acted justly and rightly¦348188, will certainly¦348190 live.
\p
\v 17 But¦348193 your people¦348195 say, “The master's¦348199 way isn't¦348196 fair!” but¦348193 it's your ways that aren't¦348196 fair.
\v 18 When the righteous \add >person\add* turns¦348206 away from \add ≈doing what's right\add* and \add ≈becomes disobedient\add*, then they'll die¦348212 in¦348213 \add @their¦348213 sin¦348213\add*.
\v 19 When¦348215 the wicked¦348216 \add >person\add* turns¦348215 away from \add ≈doing evil\add* and does what's just and right¦348220, they'll live¦348223 because of those things.
\v 20 \add ≈Yes,\add* you people say¦348225, “The master's¦348229 way¦348228 isn't¦348226 fair!” \add but¦348225\add* I'll¦348232 judge¦348232 each of you \add ≈Israeli¦348234 people\add* according¦348231 to \add ≈your behaviour\add*.”
\s1 The demolition of Yerushalem
\rem /s1 Explanation of Jerusalem's Fall; The News of Jerusalem's Fall; The Fall of Jerusalem; Jerusalem's Fall Explained
\p
\v 21 In \add ≈mid-January\add* of the twelfth year¦348241 of our¦348245 captivity \add in¦348239 Babylon\add*, \add ≈someone who'd escaped¦348249\add* from Yerushalem¦348250 \add (Jerusalem)\add* came¦348246 to me and¦348238 told me that the city¦348253 had been captured.\x + \xo 33:21: \xt 2Ki 25:3-10; Jer 39:2-8; 52:4-14.\x*
\v 22 Yahweh¦348257 had \add ≈held me in¦348260 a vision\add* the evening¦348260 before the fugitive¦348263 came, \add ≈but¦348255\add* my mouth was opened by the time he came to me at¦348260 dawn, \add ≈so by then¦348255, I¦348276 was able to speak again¦348277\add*.
\s1 More desolation to come
\rem /s1 The Sins of the People; The Survivors in Judah
\p
\v 23 Then Yahweh¦348283 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦348281:
\v 24 “Humanity's child, the people inhabiting¦348290 those ruins¦348291 \add back\add* in Yisrael¦348296 \add (Israel¦348296)\add* are saying¦348297, ‘Abraham¦348301 was only one person, and he inherited the land, \add ≈but¦348302 there's lots of us\add* \add now\add*. The land has been given¦348309 to us as an inheritance.’
\p
\v 25 Therefore tell them that the master¦348321 Yahweh¦348321 says this: You eat blood, and you \add ≈pay your respect to\add* your idols¦348332, then you pour out people's blood. Should you then inherit that land?
\v 26 You \add people\add* \add ≈rely\add* on your swords¦348341 \add for protection\add*, and you've done¦348342 disgusting things—each man defiles¦348349 his neighbour's¦348348 wife¦348347. Should you \add really\add* possess¦348351 that land?’
\p
\v 27 Tell them that¦348381 the master¦348361 Yahweh¦348361 says¦348360 this: As I live, surely the ones in the ruins¦348370 will be killed by swords¦348371, and I'll¦348379 give¦348379 those in the countryside¦348377 to the \add ≈wild animals¦348378\add* as food, and those in fortresses and in caves¦348383 will die¦348385 of plagues¦348384.
\v 28 Then¦348387 I'll¦348387 turn the \add ≈country\add* into a desolation¦348391 and a horror, and the pride¦348394 \add ≈that came from its¦348395 power\add* will end¦348393, because Yisrael's¦348398 mountains¦348397 will be deserted¦348396, and there'll be no one to \add ≈walk\add* through them.’
\v 29 Then¦348402 they'll know¦348402 that¦348417 I am Yahweh¦348406, when I make¦348407 the \add ≈country\add* a desolation¦348411 and¦348402 a wasteland¦348412 because¦348403 of all the \add ≈disgusting things\add* they've done¦348418.
\s1 Listening but not obeying
\rem /s1 The Results of the Prophet's Message
\p
\v 30 So now humanity's¦348424 child¦348425, your¦348426 people¦348426 are out beside¦348429 the walls¦348430 and the gates of their houses¦348432 talking¦348427 about you¦348428, and they're all telling each other, ‘Let's go and listen¦348447 to the prophet's message¦348449 that¦348450 comes¦348450 from Yahweh¦348452.’
\v 31 So¦348454 my people will come to you, as they often do, and will sit in¦348472 front¦348460 of you and listen¦348462 to your words, but they won't¦348467 obey them. The right words are \add ≈on¦348472 their lips\add*, but their hearts¦348477 are going¦348478 after¦348475 unjust¦348476 profit.
\v 32 \add ≈Yes,\add* you're like a lovely song to them, a beautiful¦348484 sound that's played well on a stringed instrument, so they'll listen¦348488 to your¦348491 words¦348491, but¦348480 none of them will obey them.
\v 33 So¦348496 when all that happens, watch out, \add because¦348500 it¦348498 will indeed\add* happen. Then¦348496 they'll \add ≈realise\add* that a prophet's¦348501 been among¦348503 them.”
\c 34
\s1 The ‘shepherds’ of Yisrael (Israel)
\rem /s1 The Shepherds of Israel; Israel's False Shepherds; Shepherds and Sheep
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦348509 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦348507:
\v 2 “Humanity's child, prophesy against Yisrael's shepherds. Prophesy and tell them that the master¦348529 Yahweh¦348529 says this to the shepherds: Yisrael's shepherds who are shepherding themselves¦348538 \add ≈won't¦348539 end well\add*. Shouldn't shepherds guard the flock¦348540?
\v 3 You eat¦348547 the \add ≈best sheep¦348552 meat\add* and¦348548 dress in \add ≈woollen clothes¦348551\add*. You slaughter¦348553 the \add ≈fat lambs\add*. You don't¦348555 shepherd¦348556 at all.
\v 4 You haven't \add ≈taken care of those\add* with diseases, nor do you heal those who are ill. You don't bandage up the ones \add ≈with broken limbs\add*, and you don't restore the outcasts or \add ≈search for those who've gone missing\add*. Instead, you use strength¦348562 and violence to rule over them.
\v 5 Then¦348587 they were scattered without a shepherd¦348589, and¦348587 after they were scattered, they became¦348590 food for all the \add ≈wild animals¦348594\add* in the countryside¦348595.\x + \xo 34:5: \xt Num 27:17; 1Ki 22:17; Mat 9:36; Mrk 6:34.\x*
\v 6 My flock has strayed across all the mountains¦348602 and onto every¦348604 high¦348607 hill, and it's dispersed over the entire¦348600 surface¦348611 of the earth¦348612, yet no one is searching¦348616 for them.
\p
\v 7 Therefore¦348621, you shepherds¦348621, listen¦348622 to Yahweh's¦348626 message¦348625 \add to you\add*:
\v 8 This is the master¦348633 Yahweh's¦348634 declaration: As I live, my flock has become plunder and food for all the wild animals¦348649 in the countryside¦348650, because there¦348651 wasn't any shepherd and \add ≡because\add* none of my shepherds \add ≈tried to find any of\add* my flock \add that had gotten lost\add*. \add ≈Instead it was themselves¦348662 that\add* the shepherds looked after, and they didn't¦348637 shepherd my flock.
\v 9 Therefore¦348671, \add you so-called\add* ‘shepherds’¦348671, listen¦348672 to Yahweh's¦348675 message¦348673:
\v 10 The master¦348680 Yahweh¦348680 says this: Listen, I'm against¦348683 those shepherds. I'll require \add ≈compensation from them for\add* my flock, and then I'll dismiss them from shepherding the flock. Those shepherds won't shepherd themselves¦348699 any longer \add either\add*, \add ≈because\add* I'll take my flock away from their¦348702 mouths¦348702, so that my flock won't become¦348705 food for them any longer.
\s1 The evil shepherds
\rem /s1 God, the True Shepherd; The Good Shepherd
\p
\v 11 \add ≈So\add* the master¦348713 Yahweh¦348713 says¦348712 this: Listen, I myself will search¦348718 for \add those lost from\add* my flock¦348721, and I \add ≡myself\add* will look after them,
\v 12 like a shepherd¦348725 searches for his sheep¦348732 when he's among¦348730 his scattered flock. So I'll look for my flock and I'll rescue¦348739 them from all the places¦348743 where¦348744 they¦348745 were scattered \add ≈during the dark¦348749 times\add*.
\v 13 I'll bring \add @my people¦348754\add* out from among the \add ≈nations\add*. I'll gather¦348755 them from \add foreign\add* lands¦348758 and bring them \add back\add* to their¦348762 land. I'll put them in pastures on¦348768 Yisrael's mountainsides¦348766, by the streams, and in every settlement in the land.
\v 14 I'll¦348776 put them in good pastures—Yisrael's high mountains¦348778 will be their¦348783 grazing places. They'll lie down there¦348784 in places good for grazing, in abundant pastures, and they'll graze¦348790 on¦348778 Yisrael's hills.
\v 15 I myself will shepherd¦348797 my flock¦348798, and¦348799 I myself will \add ≈give them rest\add*. That's the master¦348802 Yahweh's¦348803 declaration.
\p
\v 16 I'll search¦348808 out the lost and restore the outcast. I'll bandage up those who are broken and heal those who are sick¦348817, but¦348809 I'll destroy¦348825 those who are fat and well. I will shepherd¦348826 with¦348827 justice¦348827.
\p
\v 17 So¦348829 now this is what the master¦348833 Yahweh¦348833 says, You, my flock¦348830, listen, I'll be a judge¦348836 between one sheep and another, and between rams and billy goats¦348842.
\v 18 Isn't it enough¦348844 to feed on¦348852 the good pasture? Do you have to trample¦348851 down \add ≈any remaining grass\add*? Or drink¦348856 from clear¦348853 waters¦348855, then muddy¦348860 the streams with¦348852 your feet?
\v 19 Must my flock¦348862 eat what you've trampled¦348863 and¦348862 drink¦348868 what you've muddied with your feet?
\p
\v 20 Therefore the master¦348874 Yahweh¦348874 says this to them: Listen, I myself will judge¦348880 between the fat sheep and the thin ones,
\v 21 because you \add shepherds\add* have pushed them with your sides and shoulders¦348891, and you've gored all the weak ones with your horns¦348893 until¦348898 you \add succeeded in¦348890\add* scattering¦348900 them away from the land.
\v 22 I'll save my flock¦348907 and they'll no longer be \add taken as\add* plunder, and I'll judge¦348913 between¦348914 one sheep and \add ≈the next\add*.
\v 23 I'll¦348918 set one shepherd over them—my servant¦348925 David¦348926. He'll \add ≈look after\add* them, and¦348918 he'll be their shepherd.\x + \xo 34:23: \xt Rev 7:17.\x*
\v 24 Then¦348936 I, Yahweh, will be their¦348944 god¦348940, and¦348941 my servant¦348941 David¦348942 will be a leader among¦348944 them. I, Yahweh, have declared that.\x + \xo 34:24: \xt Eze 37:24.\x*
\rem /s1 The Lord's Covenant of Peace
\p
\v 25 Then¦348949 I'll¦348953 make¦348949 a peace¦348952 treaty with¦348961 them and remove the wild¦348956 animals¦348954 from the land, so that they'll \add be able to\add* live¦348960 securely¦348962 in the wilderness¦348961 and sleep¦348963 safely in the forests¦348964.
\p
\v 26 I'll¦348966 also \add ≈bless\add* \add both\add* them and the places around¦348968 my hill, and send¦348971 out showers in¦348973 the right season¦348973—they'll be showers of blessing.
\v 27 Then¦348978 the trees in the countryside¦348980 will produce their fruit¦348983, the soil will yield its¦348986 produce, and my sheep will be secure¦348991 in their land¦348990. When¦348978 I break¦348997 \add ≈off the ties holding the yoke\add* \add onto their necks\add*, and when I rescue¦349002 them from the \add ≈control\add* of those¦349004 who enslaved¦349004 them, then¦348978 they'll know¦348992 that I am Yahweh¦348996.
\v 28 They'll no longer be plunder for the \add other\add* nations¦349012, and the wild animals¦349013 on the land will no longer devour¦349016 them. Then¦349007 they'll live¦349017 securely¦349018, and no one will \add ≈make them afraid\add*.
\v 29 I'll¦349022 provide¦349022 a land for them that's known for its crops, so they won't be victims of famine¦349031, and¦349022 they won't be \add ≈mocked by\add* \add other\add* nations¦349038.
\v 30 Then¦349040 they'll know that I, Yahweh their¦349044 god¦349044, am with them. The \add ≈Israeli¦349048 people\add* are my people. That's the master¦349051 Yahweh's¦349043 declaration.
\p
\v 31 You are my sheep¦349055, the flock¦349056 of my pasture¦349057, and my people, and I am your¦349061 god¦349061. That's the master¦349063 Yahweh's¦349064 declaration.’ ”
\c 35
\s1 Edom's coming punishment
\rem /s1 A Prophecy Against Edom; God's Punishment of Edom; A Message for Edom; Judgment on Mount Seir
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦349070 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦349068:\x + \xo 35:1-15: \xt Isa 34:5-17; 63:1-6; Jer 49:7-22; Eze 25:12-14; Amos 1:11-12; Oba 1-14; Mal 1:2-5.\x*
\v 2 “Humanity's child, \add ≈look out towards\add* Mt¦349081. Seir¦349082 and¦349083 prophesy¦349083 against it.
\v 3 Tell it that the master¦349090 Yahweh¦349090 says¦349089 this: Listen, I'm against you \add Edom, represented by\add* Mt¦349094. Seir¦349096, and I'll¦349097 \add ≈work against you to\add* make¦349100 you a desolation¦349101 and a waste¦349102.
\v 4 I'll¦349106 \add ≈turn\add* your¦349104 cities into ruins, and you yourself will become desolate¦349108. Then you'll know¦349110 that I am Yahweh¦349114.
\p
\v 5 You've¦349126 always been hostile to the Israeli¦349124 people¦349124, and when they were distressed you \add ≈decided to attack them with¦349132 swords¦349131\add*—at¦349132 the time their¦349133 punishment¦349135 was coming to an end.
\v 6 Therefore, this is the master¦349143 Yahweh's¦349144 declaration: As I live, I'll¦349148 prepare¦349148 you for bloodshed, and bloodshed will chase after you. Since you didn't¦349153 hate bloodshed, bloodshed will \add ≈come to\add* you.
\v 7 I'll¦349159 make¦349159 \add ≈Edom\add* completely desolate¦349165 when I cut¦349166 \add ≈it off so that no one will want to pass through¦349168 it\add*.
\v 8 I'll¦349171 fill¦349171 its mountain¦349174 \add ranges\add* with¦349185 people who were killed—those¦349181 killed by the sword¦349183 will fall¦349184 on¦349185 your high hills¦349176 and valleys¦349177 and in¦349185 all your streams.
\v 9 I'll¦349189 make¦349189 \add ≈your¦349190 land perpetually¦349188 desolate\add*. Your cities¦349190 won't¦349191 be inhabited, and then you'll know¦349194 that I am Yahweh¦349198.
\p
\v 10 You've said¦349201, “These two nations¦349205 and these two lands¦349209 will become mine, and we'll possess¦349212 them,” even when Yahweh¦349213 was there¦349214 with them.
\v 11 Therefore, this is the master¦349222 Yahweh's¦349223 declaration: As I live, I'll act¦349224 according to your anger¦349225 and according to your jealousy¦349226 that¦349227 you had in¦349230 your hatred¦349229 of Yisrael, and I'll show myself¦349231 to them when I judge¦349234 you—
\v 12 then¦349236 you'll know¦349236 that¦349248 I am Yahweh¦349240. I've¦349241 heard¦349241 all the insults you spoke against Yisrael's \add ≈hill country\add*, when you said¦349249, “They've been destroyed—they've been given¦349259 over to us to devour.”
\v 13 You raised yourselves¦349262 up against me with¦349264 what you said, and you multiplied¦349265 the words¦349267 you said against me, and I heard¦349269 all of them.
\p
\v 14 The¦349275 master¦349274 Yahweh¦349274 says¦349273 this: I'll¦349281 make¦349281 you¦349283 \add ≈into a desolate¦349280 place\add* while the \add ≈rest of the world cheers\add*.
\v 15 Just like you rejoiced¦349285 over the Israeli¦349287 people's inheritance¦349286 \add ≈when it was made desolate\add*, I'll¦349295 do¦349295 the same to you. You'll become a desolation, Mt¦349300. Seir¦349302 and all of Edom¦349305—all of it. Then they'll know¦349307 that I am Yahweh¦349311.”
\c 36
\s1 God's blessing for Yisrael (Israel)
\rem /s1 Blessing on Israel; A Prophecy to the Mountains of Israel; Restoration for Israel; God's Blessing on Israel
\p
\v 1 “Now, humanity's¦349317 child, prophesy¦349318 to Yisrael's mountains and say¦349323, ‘Mountains of Yisrael, listen¦349326 to Yahweh's¦349329 message¦349327.
\v 2 The master¦349333 Yahweh¦349333 says¦349332 this: The enemy¦349337 has said¦349336, “Aha,” \add when talking\add* about you¦349338, and¦349340 “The ancient \add ≈hilltop¦349340 shrines¦349340\add* have become¦349343 ours.” ’
\p
\v 3 Therefore prophesy¦349347 and tell them that the master¦349351 Yahweh¦349351 says¦349350 this: Because of your desolation¦349355 and because¦349353 of the attacks that came on¦349354 you from all sides¦349358, you've \add ≈ended up belonging to\add* other nations¦349362—you've been the subject of slanderous¦349368 lips and tongues¦349367, and of people's¦349370 stories.
\v 4 Therefore, mountains of Yisrael¦349374, listen¦349375 to the message¦349376 from the master Yahweh: The master Yahweh says this to the mountains and the high hills¦349386, to the streambeds and valleys¦349388, to the \add ≈places¦349389 that have been destroyed and are now uninhabited\add*, and to the forsaken cities¦349391 that have become¦349394 plunder¦349395 and a subject of mocking for the other nations¦349398 that surround them—
\v 5 the master¦349407 Yahweh¦349407 says¦349406 this: I've¦349414 certainly spoken¦349414 in the \add ≈heat\add* of my anger against the other nations¦349418, against Edom¦349421 and everyone¦349433 who took my land for themselves as a possession¦349431—against all those who had both celebration in their heart¦349435 and disdain in their spirit as they seized my land so they could claim its¦349439 pasturelands¦349439 for themselves.’
\p
\v 6 Therefore, prophesy¦349443 to Yisrael¦349447 and tell the mountains¦349449 and the high hills¦349450, the streambeds and the valleys¦349452, that the master¦349457 Yahweh¦349457 says¦349455 this: Listen, in my fury and in my anger¦349461 I'm declaring this because¦349463 you've¦349466 endured insults¦349464 from \add other\add* nations¦349465.
\v 7 Therefore, the master¦349471 Yahweh¦349471 says¦349470 this: I myself will \add ≈take action and promise\add* that the nations¦349481 that surround you¦349483 will certainly \add be made to\add* carry their¦349486 own shame.
\v 8 But¦349490 you Israeli mountains¦349491, you'll grow branches¦349493 and¦349495 bear fruit¦349495 for my Israeli people¦349497, since they'll soon come¦349501 back to you,
\v 9 because, listen, I'm¦349504 for you, and I'll¦349506 treat you favourably—you'll be ploughed and sown \add with seed\add*.
\v 10 I'll¦349511 multiply¦349511 \add ≈your population—all the Israeli¦349517 people\add*. The cities¦349520 will be inhabited¦349519 \add again\add* and¦349511 the ruins¦349521 rebuilt¦349522.
\v 11 I'll increase¦349528 \add both\add* people and animals¦349527 on you \add ≡mountains\add* so that they'll multiply¦349524 and be fruitful¦349529. Then¦349524 I'll cause¦349530 you to be inhabited¦349530 like you were previously, and I'll make you prosper more than you did in the past \add ≈so that\add* you'll know¦349535 that I am Yahweh¦349539.
\v 12 I'll¦349541 bring my Israeli people¦349546 to walk¦349541 on you. They'll possess¦349548 you, and you'll be their inheritance¦349551, and you'll no longer cause¦349541 their children to die.
\p
\v 13 The master¦349561 Yahweh¦349561 says¦349560 this: They're telling you, “You \add ≡mountains\add* devour¦349566 people¦349564, bereaving¦349570 your nation¦349571 of children,”
\v 14 therefore you won't¦349577 consume people any longer, and you'll no longer make your nation¦349581 mourn their deaths. That's the master¦349589 Yahweh's¦349590 declaration.
\v 15 I won't¦349592 allow you to hear the insults¦349597 from the \add other\add* nations¦349598 any longer—you'll no longer have to bear the people¦349600 groups shaming you, and¦349592 you won't cause¦349609 your nation¦349605 to stumble¦349609. That's the master¦349612 Yahweh's¦349613 declaration.”
\rem /s1 The Renewal of Israel; Israel's New Life
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh¦349619 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦349617:
\v 17 “Humanity's child, when the \add ≈Israeli¦349626 people\add* inhabited their land¦349631, they defiled¦349632 it with¦349634 their customs and their activities. \add ≈In my¦349640 eyes,\add* their customs were like the uncleanness¦349636 of a woman's menstruation¦349637,
\v 18 \add ≈so\add* I poured out my fury against them for the blood¦349647 that¦349648 they'd poured out on¦349654 the land, and¦349642 for how they'd polluted it¦349655 with¦349654 their¦349654 idols¦349654.
\v 19 I scattered¦349657 them among the nations¦349659—they¦349660 were dispersed¦349660 through the lands¦349661. I judged¦349664 them according to their customs and¦349657 their actions.
\v 20 Then¦349666 they went to those \add other\add* nations¦349669, and¦349666 wherever¦349670 they went, they profaned¦349674 my holy name¦349677 when people asked about them, ‘Are these¦349684 really Yahweh's¦349683 people? After all, they've been thrown out of his land¦349685.’
\v 21 \add ≈But¦349688\add* I¦349696 had compassion for my holy name¦349691 that¦349693 the \add ≈Israeli¦349695 people\add* had defiled among¦349697 the \add other\add* nations¦349697, when they¦349700 went there¦349701.
\p
\v 22 Therefore tell the \add ≈Israeli people\add* that¦349725 the master¦349711 Yahweh¦349711 says¦349710 this: I'm not doing this for the sake of you \add ≈Israeli people\add*, but for my sacred¦349724 name¦349722, which¦349725 you've profaned¦349726 among¦349727 the nations¦349727 everywhere you've gone.
\v 23 My great¦349737 name¦349736, which¦349740 you \add people\add* have profaned among the nations, I'll¦349733 make holy \add even\add* among the nations \add where\add* you profaned it. Then¦349733 the nations will know that¦349740 I am Yahweh, when they see you recognise my holiness¦349733. That's the master¦349750 Yahweh's¦349748 declaration.
\v 24 I'll take¦349756 you \add back\add* out of those nations¦349760 and gather¦349761 you from every land, and I'll bring¦349766 you into your land.
\v 25 Then¦349772 I'll sprinkle¦349772 pure water¦349774 on you so you'll be cleaned¦349776 from all of your impurities¦349778, and¦349772 I'll purify¦349782 you from all your idols¦349781.
\v 26 I'll give you \add people\add* a new heart and a new spirit¦349789 in¦349792 your innermost¦349792 parts¦349792, and I'll take the stony heart away from your \add ≈bodies\add* and \add ≈replace it with¦349792\add* a heart of flesh.\x + \xo 36:26-28: \xt Eze 11:19-20.\x*
\v 27 I'll¦349807 put my spirit in¦349808 you and enable you \add Israelis¦349809\add* to \add ≈follow my instructions¦349813 and keep¦349816 my regulations¦349813\add*.
\v 28 Then¦349819 you'll inhabit the land that¦349821 I gave¦349822 to your ancestors¦349823—you'll be my people¦349826 and¦349819 I'll¦349828 be your god¦349830.
\v 29 I'll save¦349832 you from all of your uncleanness¦349835, and then¦349832 I'll summon¦349836 the grain¦349839 and multiply it—I'll no longer \add ≈assign\add* famine¦349846 to you.
\v 30 I'll¦349848 multiply the \add #fruit on¦349862 the trees\add* and¦349853 the produce¦349853 \add ≈from the soil\add* so that you'll no longer \add ≈be shamed by other nations¦349862 seeing you suffering\add* famine¦349861.
\v 31 Then¦349864 you'll think about your wicked customs¦349867 and your activities that¦349870 weren't good¦349873, and \add ≈your faces¦349875 will display your loathing¦349874 of your own sins and your disgusting habits\add*.
\v 32 But¦349892 make sure you know this: I'm not doing all that for \em your\em* sake, \add ≈so you Israeli¦349894 people\add* should¦349889 be ashamed¦349891 and¦349892 disgraced because of your \add ≈behaviour\add*. That's the master¦349887 Yahweh's¦349888 declaration.
\p
\v 33 The master¦349900 Yahweh¦349900 says¦349899 this: \add ≈When\add* I purify you from all your disobedience, I'll¦349907 enable you to inhabit¦349907 the cities¦349910 \add again\add* and to rebuild¦349911 the ruined places¦349912,
\v 34 and¦349914 the idle land will be tilled¦349916 until it's no longer a wasteland for everyone¦349922 to see as they go past.
\v 35 Then¦349926 \add instead\add* they'll say, “This land was desolate¦349935, but¦349926 it's become¦349930 like the garden¦349931 of Eden¦349933—the abandoned cities and the uninhabited ruins that were torn down are now fortified¦349938 and inhabited.”
\v 36 Then¦349941 the other nations¦349942 around¦349945 you¦349945 will know¦349941 that¦349943 I am Yahweh, because¦349946 I built up the ruins and replanted the abandoned places¦349953. I, Yahweh, have declared it and I will do it.
\p
\v 37 The master¦349962 Yahweh¦349962 \add also\add* says¦349961 this: The \add ≈Israeli¦349969 people\add* will again¦349964 ask me to do this for them: to increase¦349972 \add ≈their population like sheep\add*.
\v 38 Just like the flock \add ≈that's kept separate to be used\add* for sacrifices (like the flock in¦349981 Yerushalem¦349980 at¦349981 its¦349981 scheduled feasts¦349981), so too the ruined¦349985 cities¦349984 will be filled with¦349981 flocks of people and¦349989 they¦349983 will know¦349989 that I am Yahweh¦349993.”
\c 37
\s1 The valley with bleached bones
\rem /s1 A Valley of Dry Bones; The Valley of Dry Bones
\p
\v 1 \add ≈One¦350003 day Yahweh¦350000 gave me another vision\add*, and he took me out by his spirit¦350002 and put me down in¦350002 the middle¦350005 of a valley¦350006 that was full of bones¦350009.
\v 2 Then¦350011 he led me all around them, and wow, there were so many bones¦350017 in the valley¦350022, and they were totally dried out!
\v 3 “Humanity's child,” he \add ≈asked\add* me, “can these¦350034 bones¦350033 become living \add people\add* again?”
\p “My master¦350036 Yahweh¦350036,” I \add ≈replied\add*, “\add ≈only you would know¦350039 that\add*.”
\p
\v 4 Then¦350041 he told me, “Prophesy over these¦350047 bones and¦350041 tell them, ‘Dry bones. Listen to Yahweh's¦350055 message¦350053.
\v 5 The master¦350059 Yahweh¦350059 says¦350058 this to these¦350062 bones¦350061: Listen, I'm about¦350065 to put breath¦350067 into you, and¦350068 you'll live¦350068.
\v 6 I'll \add ≈connect\add* sinews to you and put flesh¦350075 onto you. I'll cover¦350076 you with¦350080 skin and put breath¦350081 into you so you'll live¦350082. Then¦350070 you'll know¦350083 that I am Yahweh¦350087.’ ”
\p
\v 7 So¦350089 I prophesied¦350089 as I was instructed¦350091, and¦350089 wow, a sound¦350094 like something rattling¦350098 started as the bones¦350100 moved together¦350099—bone against bone!
\v 8 I¦350107 looked¦350106 and, wow, the bones now had sinews on¦350119 them, and flesh¦350111 grew up \add over that\add*, and then¦350106 skin covered¦350113 them! \add ≈But¦350106\add* there¦350118 was still no breath in¦350119 them.
\p
\v 9 Then¦350121 Yahweh told me, “Prophesy to the wind. Humanity's child, prophesy and tell the air, ‘The master¦350140 Yahweh \add ≈commands\add* this: Breath, come from the four \add ≈compass points\add*, and breathe¦350146 into these¦350148 who've been killed¦350147, so they'll \add ≈come to life\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦350151\add* I prophesied¦350151 just like I was commanded¦350153. Then breath¦350156 came into them and¦350151 they \add ≈came to life¦350157\add*. Then they stood¦350158 on their¦350161 feet¦350161—a very large¦350163 army.
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh \add ≈informed\add* me, “Humanity's child, these¦350175 bones are the entire¦350176 \add ≈Israeli¦350178 population\add*. Listen, they're saying¦350182, ‘Our bones have dried¦350183 up and our hope is gone. We've been cut¦350187 off.’
\v 12 Therefore prophesy¦350191 and tell them, ‘The master¦350197 Yahweh¦350197 says this: Listen, I'll open your graves and lift you, my people¦350208, out from them, then I'll bring you back to Yisrael¦350214.
\v 13 Then, my people¦350229, when I open¦350222 your graves and bring¦350226 you out of them, you'll know¦350217 that I am Yahweh¦350221.
\v 14 I'll place¦350235 my spirit within you so you'll live¦350234, and I'll cause you to rest in¦350233 your land¦350239 when you know¦350240 that I am Yahweh. I declare it, and I will do it. That is Yahweh's¦350244 declaration.’ ”
\s1 The two sticks indicating unity of Yisrael and Yehudah
\rem /s1 One Nation Under One King; The Two Sticks; Reunion of Israel and Judah; Judah and Israel in One Kingdom
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh¦350255 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦350253:
\v 16 “\add ≈So¦350259 now\add*, humanity's¦350262 child, take a stick for yourself¦350265 and write on it, ‘For Yehudah¦350270 and for all their associated people.’ Then¦350259 take another stick and write on it, ‘For \add Yisrael¦350272\add* (\add represented by\add* Yosef¦350280 \add (Joseph)\add*, the branch of Efrayim¦350282), and for all their associated people.’
\v 17 Then¦350290 hold both of them together so that they¦350299 \add ≈seem like\add* one stick¦350297 in¦350301 your¦350301 hand¦350301.
\v 18 When¦350303 your people¦350307 \add ≈ask you and¦350303 insist\add*, ‘Aren't you going to tell us what these¦350315 props of yours mean?’
\v 19 then tell them, ‘The master¦350323 Yahweh¦350323 says this: Look, I'm taking Yosef's branch that's in Efrayim's¦350335 hand along with¦350333 the tribes¦350336 of Yisrael¦350337 his¦350338 companions¦350338, and joining it to Yehudah¦350346's branch, so that they'll make one branch, and they'll become one in my hand.’
\p
\v 20 Hold the branches in¦350360 your¦350360 hand¦350360 that¦350356 you¦350358 wrote on¦350360 \add ≈while they¦350354 watched\add*.
\v 21 Tell \add @the people\add*, ‘The master¦350368 Yahweh¦350368 says this: Look, I'm about¦350372 to take the Israeli¦350375 people out from the nations¦350378 where¦350379 they went. I'll¦350383 gather¦350383 them from the surrounding lands and¦350363 bring¦350386 them \add back\add* to their¦350390 land¦350390.
\v 22 I'll¦350392 \add ≈unite them as a single\add* nation in¦350397 the land, on¦350397 the mountains¦350397 of Yisrael¦350398, and there'll be one king as king over all of them, and they'll no longer be two nations—they'll no longer be divided¦350412 into two kingdoms¦350415.
\v 23 Then¦350418 they'll no longer defile themselves¦350419 with their idols¦350421, with their disgusting things¦350422, or with any of their other sins. I'll save¦350425 them from all of their faithless actions\f + \fr 37:23 \ft The Hebrew text has ‘living places’ rather than ‘faithless actions’ (from the Septuagint), but it's difficult to make sense of the Hebrew.\f* with which¦350429 they've sinned¦350430, and I'll purify them so they'll be my people¦350437 and I'll be their god¦350441.
\v 24 My servant¦350443 David¦350444 will be king¦350445 over them, \add ≈so\add* there'll be one shepherd¦350447 over them all, and they'll \add ≈follow\add* my instructions¦350453 and keep¦350454 my regulations¦350453 and obey them.\x + \xo 37:24: \xt Eze 34:24.\x*
\v 25 They'll live in the land that¦350462 I've given to my servant Yakov \add (Jacob, also called Yisrael/Israel)\add*, where your ancestors¦350470 had stayed. They'll live in it forever¦350484—they, their children¦350474, and their grandchildren, and my servant David¦350480 will be their \add ≈permanent¦350479\add* leader.
\v 26 I'll establish a peace¦350489 treaty with them—a never-ending agreement¦350488 with them. I'll establish them and multiply¦350495 them and put my sacred place among¦350501 them forever¦350502.
\v 27 My residence will be with them—I'll¦350507 be their god¦350509, and¦350504 they'll be my people¦350514.\x + \xo 37:27: \xt 2Cor 6:16; Rev 21:3.\x*
\v 28 Then¦350516 the \add other\add* nations¦350517 will know¦350516 that I¦350519 am Yahweh¦350520 who sets Yisrael¦350524 apart, when my sacred place is \add ≈permanently\add* among¦350527 them.’ ”
\c 38
\ms1 The defeat of all of Yisrael's opponents
\mr (38:1–39:29)
\s1 Invasion by Gog and his army
\rem /s1 Gog as the Instrument of God; A Message for Gog; A Prophecy Against Gog; Invasion by Gog
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦350534 \add ≈gave\add* me \add another\add* message¦350532:
\v 2 “Humanity's child, \add ≈look\add* toward the land¦350546 of Magog¦350547 \add ?where King\add* Gog¦350545 is the main leader of Meshek¦350550 and Tuval, and prophesy¦350552 against¦350543 him.\x + \xo 38:2: \xt Rev 20:8.\x*
\v 3 Tell him that the master¦350558 Yahweh¦350558 says¦350557 this: Listen, I'm against¦350561 you¦350561, Gog¦350562, leader of Meshek¦350565 and¦350566 Tuval,
\v 4 \add ≈so\add* I'll turn¦350568 you around¦350568 and put hooks¦350570 into your jaws. Then¦350568 I'll bring¦350572 you out with all your army, horses¦350580, and horsemen¦350581, all of them dressed in¦350571 full armour—a huge¦350586 group with large¦350586 and small shields, all of them holding swords¦350590.
\v 5 Persia, \add Ethiopia\add* (Kush), and Libya are with them, all of them with shields¦350598 and helmets¦350599,
\v 6 \add as well as\add* Gomer¦350601 and all its troops, and Beyt-Togarmah¦350606, from the far parts¦350607 of the north¦350608, and all its troops. Many people¦350614 groups are with you.
\v 7 \add Tell Gog to\add* \add ≈get ready as commander, and to assemble¦350624 and prepare¦350618 his troops\add*.
\v 8 You'll be \add ≈summoned many days¦350631 later\add*, then after some years¦350635 you'll go¦350636 to a land that's recovered from war and \add ≈where the people have come back from many nations\add*—gathered¦350644 back to Yisrael's mountains¦350648 that had continuously¦350654 been a ruin. But¦350655 the land's people will \add indeed\add* be brought¦350657 out of the nations, and they'll all live \add there\add* safely \add again\add*.
\v 9 Then¦350662 you'll go¦350664 \add ≈in like a storm¦350663 with your¦350672 large army\add*—you'll all be like a cloud¦350665 covering¦350666 the land.
\p
\v 10 The¦350681 master¦350680 Yahweh¦350680 says¦350679 this: \add But¦350682\add* plans will \add start to\add* form in your¦350690 heart¦350690 on that day¦350684 and you'll devise¦350691 wicked schemes.
\v 11 Then¦350695 you'll \add ≈think\add*, ‘I'll¦350696 \add take my army\add* up onto the open ground—\add ≈we'll attack\add* the people living quietly \add ≈and peacefully¦350702\add*—all of them living where there¦350711 are no walls or barriers, and no city gates¦350710.
\v 12 I'll capture booty and steal plunder \add ≈and use my forces\add* against those ruins that¦350727 have been newly inhabited¦350723, and against those people who've returned from the nations¦350728—people who are gaining livestock¦350730 and property¦350731, and who are living at the centre¦350735 of the world.’
\v 13 \add People from\add* Sheva and Dedan¦350739, and traders¦350740 from Tarshish¦350741 and its¦350744 villages¦350744 will \add ≈ask\add* you, ‘Have you come to plunder? Have you assembled¦350753 your armies to take away spoil¦350752, to carry¦350755 off gold¦350758 and silver¦350757, to take their livestock¦350760 and property¦350761 and to haul away plenty of plunder?’
\p
\v 14 Therefore, humanity's¦350771 child, prophesy¦350768 and¦350772 tell Gog¦350773 that the master¦350776 Yahweh¦350776 says¦350775 this: On that day¦350780, when my people¦350783 Yisrael¦350784 are living¦350782 securely¦350785\add ≈—but¦350772 you¦350786 won't¦350778 take notice of that—\add*
\v 15 you'll come¦350788 from your¦350789 place¦350789 far away in the north¦350791 with a large¦350800 army—all of them riding on horses¦350797, \add yes,\add* \add ≈a very numerous force\add*.
\v 16 Then¦350804 you'll attack my \add ≈Israeli people¦350807\add* like a cloud¦350809 that covers¦350810 the land. I'll¦350815 send you against my land \add ≈near the end of the era\add*, so that the nations¦350821 might know¦350820 me when I show myself¦350823 through you, Gog¦350826—\add ≈they'll see with¦350823 their¦350825 own eyes¦350825 that I always demand what's right\add*.
\rem /s1 Judgment on Gog
\v 17 The master¦350832 Yahweh¦350832 says¦350831 this: Aren't you the one that I spoke¦350839 about in the past \add ≈through\add* my servants¦350843, the Israeli¦350844 prophets who prophesied for years¦350849 in their own times, that I would bring¦350850 you against¦350852 them?
\s1 Yahweh's punishment of Gog
\rem /s1 God's Punishment of Gog
\p
\v 18 This is the master¦350867 Yahweh's¦350868 declaration: When Gog¦350861 attacks Yisrael¦350865, my fury will mount up \add ≈into a rage¦350870\add*.
\v 19 In my zeal and¦350873 in the fire¦350874 of my anger¦350876, I declare that on¦350873 that day¦350882 there'll be a terrible earthquake¦350885 in Yisrael¦350889,
\v 20 and the fish in the sea¦350894 and the birds in the skies¦350896, the wild animals¦350897 in the countryside¦350898 including¦350895 all the creatures that¦350908 crawl on the earth, and every person who's on the surface¦350911 of the land will tremble¦350891 in front of me. The mountains¦350914 will be thrown¦350913 down and the cliffs¦350916 will fall, until every wall¦350919 falls to the ground¦350920.
\v 21 I'll¦350923 summon¦350923 \add ≈an army\add* against \add @Gog\add* on¦350934 all my mountains¦350927. Every man's sword will be against his brother¦350934. That's the master¦350930 Yahweh's¦350931 declaration.
\v 22 Then¦350937 I'll \add ≈punish\add* \add @Gog\add* with plagues¦350939 and bloodshed¦350940, as well as overwhelming rain and hailstones and burning¦350945 sulfur¦350946 that I'll rain down on¦350939 him and his troops¦350951 and the many nations that \add ≈joined\add* with him.
\v 23 \add Yes,\add* I'll display¦350959 my greatness¦350959 and \add ≈purity¦350960\add*, and I'll make¦350961 myself known¦350961 to \add &those\add* many nations¦350963, \add ≈so\add* they'll know¦350965 that I am Yahweh¦350969.
\c 39
\s1 The defeat of Gog
\rem /s1 The Defeat of Gog; The Slaughter of Gog's Armies; Gog's Armies Destroyed
\p
\v 1 “Now, humanity's¦350975 child, prophesy¦350976 against Gog and say¦350980 that the master¦350983 Yahweh¦350983 says¦350982 this: Listen, I'm against you¦350986, Gog, leader of Meshek¦350990 and Tuval.
\v 2 I'll turn you around¦350993 and drag you along¦350994—I'll bring you from the far north¦350997 and take you up to Yisrael's mountains¦351001,
\v 3 Then¦351004 I'll¦351011 knock your bow out of your left hand and¦351004 make the arrows¦351008 fall out of your right¦351010 hand.
\v 4 You and all your troops¦351021 and soldiers who are with you will \add ≈die\add* on Yisrael's mountains¦351015. I'll¦351032 give¦351032 you to the birds¦351025 of prey and the wild animals¦351030 in the countryside¦351031 for food.
\v 5 You'll die \add ≈there\add* in the countryside¦351038, because¦351040 I myself declare it. That's the master¦351044 Yahweh's¦351045 declaration.
\v 6 Then¦351047 I'll¦351047 send¦351047 fire¦351049 out onto Magog¦351050 and on¦351050 those¦351051 living in safety¦351053 in the \add ≈coastal¦351052 regions\add*, and then¦351047 they'll know¦351054 that I am Yahweh¦351058.
\v 7 I'll make my holy name known¦351064 among my people¦351066 Yisrael, and¦351060 I'll no longer allow my holy name to be profaned¦351070—the nations¦351078 will know that I am Yahweh¦351082, the holy One in¦351084 Yisrael.
\p
\v 8 Listen, the day¦351093 is coming¦351087, and¦351088 \add @all that¦351094\add* will take place. That's the master¦351090 Yahweh's¦351091 declaration.
\v 9 The people living in¦351104 Yisrael's cities¦351100 will go¦351097 out and they'll use \add those\add* weapons¦351104 to kindle and make fires and burn them—small shields, large shields, bows, arrows¦351108, the clubs¦351109 and spears¦351111—they'll make fires with¦351104 them for seven¦351115 years¦351116.
\v 10 They won't¦351118 \add need to\add* gather firewood from the countryside¦351124 or cut¦351126 down trees from the forests¦351129, since they'll burn the weapons¦351131. They'll take from those \add invaders\add* who wanted to take from them, and¦351118 plunder those who wanted to plunder them. That's the master¦351144 Yahweh's¦351145 declaration.”
\s1 Gog's burial
\rem /s1 The Burial of Gog
\p
\v 11 Then \add ≈at¦351149 that time\add*, I'll¦351151 make a grave for Gog there in¦351149 Yisrael¦351158—a valley for those who journey to the east of the sea¦351162. They'll bury¦351168 Gog there with¦351149 all his multitudes¦351177, and they'll call¦351179 it the ‘Hamon-Gog’ \add (or, ‘Gog's¦351152 horde¦351177’)\add* valley. \add @That mass grave\add* will block¦351163 people¦351168 who want to cross over.
\v 12 It'll¦351186 take seven¦351192 months¦351193 for the Israelis¦351185 to bury¦351184 them to make the area clean¦351188—
\v 13 all the land's people¦351198 will bury¦351195 them. It'll be a memorable day¦351203 for them when I'm \add ≈honoured¦351202\add*. That's the master¦351206 Yahweh's¦351207 declaration.
\v 14 At the end¦351226 of the seven¦351227 months¦351229, they'll designate men¦351209 to continually¦351210 go through the \add ≈region\add* to find people¦351209 who were travelling through, but¦351209 who died and¦351209 their bodies remained¦351220 \add ≈above the ground\add*, so that they can bury¦351214 them to make¦351230 the land clean¦351225.
\v 15 As those men pass¦351232 through the land, when they see¦351235 any human¦351237 bone, they'll put a marker¦351240 by it, until¦351241 gravediggers come and¦351232 bury¦351244 it in the Hamon-Gog¦351248 valley.
\v 16 There'll even be a city¦351254 there named ‘Hamonah¦351255’ \add (which means, ‘horde’ or ‘multitude’)\add*. In that way, they'll make the land clean¦351256 \add again\add*.
\p
\v 17 Now, humanity's child, the master¦351268 Yahweh¦351268 says this to you: Tell all the winged birds¦351271 and all the wild animals¦351277 in the countryside¦351278, ‘Gather together and come¦351280. Gather from all around¦351282 to the sacrifice that I myself am making for you—a large¦351291 sacrifice on Yisrael's mountains¦351293, so that you can eat \add human\add* flesh¦351296 and drink¦351297 blood¦351298.\x + \xo 39:17-20: \xt Rev 19:17-18.\x*
\v 18 You'll eat¦351302 warriors¦351301' flesh¦351300 and drink¦351307 the blood¦351303 of the \add ≈world's leaders\add* as if they were rams, lambs¦351309, goats¦351310, and bulls¦351311 that were all fattened in Bashan¦351313.
\v 19 Then¦351316 you'll eat¦351316 fat until \add you're\add* full \add and\add* drink¦351320 blood¦351321 until \add you're\add* drunk¦351322—that'll be the sacrifice¦351323 that I'll¦351326 slaughter for you \add birds and animals\add*.
\v 20 You'll be satisfied¦351329 at my table¦351332 with \add #horses, chariot¦351334 drivers, warriors¦351335,\add* and¦351329 every man¦351338 of war¦351339. That's the master¦351341 Yahweh's¦351342 declaration.’
\s1 Yisrael's restoration
\rem /s1 Israel Restored to the Land; The Restoration of Israel
\p
\v 21 I'll \add ≈display¦351344 my power\add* among¦351348 the nations, and all the nations will see \add ≈my judgement¦351355 that I'll put into process\add* and \add ≈how I'll put that into action\add* against them.
\v 22 Then¦351366 from that \add ≈time\add* onwards¦351377, the \add ≈Israeli¦351368 people\add* will know¦351366 that I¦351370 am their¦351372 god¦351372 Yahweh¦351371.
\v 23 The nations¦351380 will know¦351379 that the \add ≈Israeli¦351386 people\add* went¦351383 into captivity because¦351381 of how they betrayed me with¦351382 their \add ≈disobedience\add*, so I \add ≈distanced myself\add* from them and¦351379 \add ≈handed them over to\add* their adversaries¦351397 so that they all \add ≈died in¦351382 the battle\add*.
\v 24 When I \add ≈distanced myself\add* from them, I \add ≈treated them exactly as their defilement and rebellion deserved\add*.
\rem /s1 Restoration for God's People
\p
\v 25 Therefore the master¦351414 Yahweh¦351414 says¦351413 this: Now I'll restore Yakov's fortunes, and I'll have compassion¦351423 on all of Yisrael's¦351427 \add ≈descendants\add*, when I act with zeal \add ≈to protect my pure¦351430 reputation¦351429\add*.
\v 26 Then¦351432 they'll bear their shame and¦351432 all the treason in which¦351441 they betrayed me. They'll forget¦351432 all that¦351441 when they rest in their land¦351448 in safety¦351449, with¦351444 no one to terrify¦351451 them
\v 27 \add when\add* I restore them from the people¦351457 groups and gather¦351458 them from their¦351461 enemies'¦351461 lands¦351460, and I show¦351462 myself¦351462 in¦351463 the sight¦351464 of many nations¦351465 \add ≈to always do what's right\add*.
\v 28 Then¦351468 they'll know that I am Yahweh¦351471 their god¦351472, because¦351473 I sent them into captivity among the \add other\add* nations, but¦351468 then¦351468 I'll¦351484 gather¦351478 them back to their \add own\add* land¦351481. I won't¦351482 leave¦351484 any of them among the nations.
\v 29 I'll¦351491 no longer \add ≈distance myself\add* from them when I pour out my spirit on the \add ≈Israeli¦351502 people\add*. That's the master¦351505 Yahweh's¦351506 declaration.”
\c 40
\ms1 The revelation concerning the coming temple and nation
\mr (40:1–48:35)
\s1 Yehezkel is taken to Yerushalem
\rem /s1 Ezekiel Is Taken to Jerusalem; The Vision of the New Temple; The new temple; The New Temple Area
\p
\v 1 In \add ≈mid-April\add* of the twenty-fifth year of our captivity (the fourteenth year after¦351520 \add @Yerushalem\add* was captured), Yahweh¦351532 \add ≈used his power to take\add* me there¦351535,
\v 2 \add yes,\add* God¦351538 took me to Yisrael¦351543 in visions¦351537. He put me on a very high¦351548 mountain¦351547 \add where\add* there seemed to be city¦351553 buildings towards the south¦351554,\x + \xo 40:2: \xt Rev 21:10.\x*
\v 3 and he took me there. Then¦351556 wow, there was a man¦351561 standing¦351572 in¦351573 the city gateway¦351573 who \add ≈looked like\add* bronze¦351564! \add ≈He¦351559 was holding\add* a linen¦351567 cord¦351565 and a measuring¦351570 rod.\x + \xo 40:3: \xt Rev 11:1; 21:15.\x*
\p
\v 4 The man¦351577 told me, “Humanity's child, look with your eyes¦351582 and listen¦351584 with your ears¦351583, and fix your mind¦351586 on¦351582 everything that I'm revealing to you, because¦351593 you were brought¦351596 here so I could reveal them to you. Report everything you see \add here\add* to the \add ≈Israeli¦351607 people\add*.”
\s1 The eastern gateway
\rem /s1 The East Gateway; The East Gate to the Outer Court; The East Gate
\p
\v 5 There was a wall surrounding the temple area. The measuring rod in¦351617 the man's hand¦351617 was \add ≈just over three metres¦351623 long\add*. (It was six¦351621 long cubits—each long cubit was a cubit long plus¦351617 a handbreadth¦351625.) \add ≈So¦351610\add* he measured the wall, and it was one measuring rod thick and one high.\x + \xo 40:5–42:20: \xt 1Ki 6:1-38; 2Ch 3:1-9.\x*
\v 6 Then¦351637 he went to the temple gate that¦351641 faced east. He went up its steps¦351646 and¦351637 measured¦351648 the gate's threshold—one rod across.
\v 7 The guard chambers¦351672 were each one rod \add ≈long and deep, and just under three metres¦351674 wide\add*. The temple gate threshold¦351675 by the temple porch¦351678 was one rod across.
\v 8 He measured¦351684 the porch¦351687 inside¦351689 the gate¦351688 and¦351684 it was one rod long,
\v 9 and¦351693 the porch over the gate was one rod deep. The doorposts were \add each\add* a metre wide. (This was the porch at the gate facing the temple.)
\v 10 There were three guard chambers¦351708 on either side of the east gate¦351709, and each of them \add ≈was the same size\add*, and the walls that separated them \add ≈were the same size\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then¦351725 the man measured¦351725 the width of the gateway entrance \add ≈and it was five metres\add*, and the length¦351734 of the gateway entrance \add ≈was almost seven metres\add*.
\v 12 He measured the wall that \add ≈ran along\add* the front¦351741 of the chambers at half a metre high. The chambers measured three metres¦351753 on each side.
\v 13 Then¦351759 he measured¦351759 the gateway from the roof of one chamber to that of the next chamber, \add ≈and¦351759 it was thirteen metres¦351769\add* from the entrance of the first chamber to that of the second.
\v 14 Then¦351774 he measured the wall that went between the guard chambers, \add ≈and¦351774 it was over thirty metres long\add* as far as the gate's porch.
\v 15 The entrance¦351792 from the front¦351796 of the gate to the other end of the gate's porch¦351797 was twenty-seven metres.
\v 16 There were closed¦351804 windows facing the chambers¦351807 and toward their¦351809 pillars¦351824 within the gates all around, and likewise for the porches¦351816. There were windows all around the inside, and each of the door jambs was decorated with \add carvings of\add* palm¦351825 trees¦351825.
\s1 The temple courtyard
\rem /s1 The Outer Courtyard; The Outer Court
\p
\v 17 Then¦351827 the man brought¦351827 me to the outer¦351831 courtyard of the temple. Look¦351832, there were rooms, and¦351827 the courtyard \add ≈was paved\add*, with thirty¦351840 rooms next to the pavement.
\v 18 The pavement \add ≈reached\add* to the side of the gates, and¦351846 its width was the same as the gates' length¦351852. That was the lower¦351855 pavement.
\v 19 Then¦351857 the man measured¦351857 the distance from the front of the lower¦351861 gate to the front of the inner¦351864 gate: it was \add ≈just over fifty metres\add* on the eastern¦351868 side, and¦351869 the same on the northern¦351869 side.
\s1 The northern gateway
\rem /s1 The North Gateway; The North Gate
\p
\v 20 Then¦351871 he measured¦351878 the length¦351879 and¦351871 width¦351880 of the gate¦351871 that¦351872 was at the northern¦351875 \add end\add* of the outer¦351877 courtyard¦351876.
\v 21 There were three chambers¦351882 on¦351902 each side of that gate, and the gate with¦351902 its porch¦351890 measured¦351893 the same as the main gate: \add ≈twenty-seven metres long and thirteen metres wide\add*.
\v 22 Its windows¦351904, porch, chambers, and its palm¦351908 trees¦351908 matched the eastern¦351915 gate¦351911. Seven steps¦351916 went up to it and to its porch.
\v 23 There was a gate to the inner¦351927 courtyard¦351926 in front of the northern¦351930 gate, just as there was at the eastern¦351931 gate. The man measured¦351932 from one gate to the other gate, \add ≈and¦351925 it was fifty-four metres\add*.
\s1 The southern gateway
\rem /s1 The South Gate; The South Gateway
\p
\v 24 Next¦351943 the man took me to the gateway at the southern entrance, and its walls and porch¦351951 measured¦351948 the same as the other outer gates.
\v 25 There were closed windows in the gateway and its porch just \add ≈like the other gates\add*. The southern gate with its porch \add ≈was twenty-seven metres long and thirteen metres wide¦351968\add*.
\v 26 There were seven¦351974 steps¦351973 up to the gate and its¦351975 porch¦351977, and \add carvings of\add* palm¦351980 trees¦351980 on the walls on each side.
\v 27 There was a gate to the inner¦351993 courtyard¦351992 on the southern side, and¦351996 the man measured¦351996 fifty-four metres¦352004 from that gate to the gate at the southern entrance.
\s1 The gateways to the inner courtyard
\rem /s1 Gates to the Inner Court; Gateways to the Inner Courtyard; The Inner Courtyard: The South Gate
\p
\v 28 Then¦352006 the man brought¦352006 me to the inner¦352010 courtyard¦352009 by way of its southern gate, which had the same measurements¦352013 as the other gates.
\v 29 Its chambers¦352021, walls, and porches measured¦352027 the same as the other gates—there were windows¦352029 all around in the porch. The inner gate with its porch \add ≈was twenty-seven metres long and thirteen metres wide¦352039\add*.
\v 30 There were also porches¦352044 all around the inner wall—they were \add all\add* \add ≈thirteen metres long and¦352044 almost three metres wide¦352052\add*.
\v 31 The porch¦352056 facing the outer¦352061 courtyard¦352060 had \add carved\add* palm¦352062 trees¦352062 on its walls, and¦352056 eight¦352068 steps¦352067 going up to it.
\s1 The eastern gate to the inner courtyard
\rem /s1 The Inner Courtyard: The East Gate
\p
\v 32 Then¦352072 the man brought¦352072 me to the inner¦352076 courtyard¦352075 by the eastern¦352078 side and¦352079 measured¦352079 the gate¦352082, which had the same measurements as the other gates.
\v 33 Its chambers¦352086, walls, and porches measured¦352092 the same as the other gates, and there were windows¦352094 all around. The inner gate with its porch \add ≈was twenty-seven metres long and thirteen metres wide¦352104\add*.
\v 34 Its porch¦352109 \add that\add* faced¦352111 the outer¦352112 courtyard¦352111 had \add carved\add* palm¦352113 trees¦352113 on each side of it, and¦352109 eight¦352120 steps¦352121 going up it.
\s1 The northern gate to the inner courtyard
\rem /s1 The Inner Courtyard: The North Gate
\p
\v 35 Next the man brought¦352125 me to the northern¦352129 gate¦352128 and measured it, \add and\add* it measured the same as the other gates.
\v 36 Its chambers, walls, and porch measured the same as the other gates, and there were windows¦352140 all around. The gateway with its porch \add ≈was twenty-seven metres long and thirteen metres wide¦352148\add*.
\v 37 Its porch \add that\add* faced¦352155 the outer¦352156 courtyard¦352155 had \add carved\add* palm¦352157 trees¦352157 on each side of it, and¦352153 eight¦352164 steps¦352165 going up it.
\s1 Rooms for preparing sacrifices
\rem /s1 Rooms for Preparing Sacrifices; Buildings Near the North Gate; The Rooms for Preparing Sacrifices
\p
\v 38 There was a room¦352169 with¦352171 a door by each of the inner gateways which was where they rinsed¦352174 the burnt¦352177 offerings¦352177.
\v 39 There were two tables on¦352179 each side of each porch¦352179, which¦352188 were for slaughtering¦352187 \add the offerings:\add* the burnt¦352189 offering, the sin¦352190 offering, and¦352179 the guilt¦352191 offering.
\v 40 By the wall of the courtyard, going up to the gate to the north¦352200, there were two tables. Also¦352193 on the other¦352206 side there were two tables at the gate's porch¦352208.
\v 41 There were four tables by the gate¦352220 on each side¦352219—they slaughtered¦352224 animals on those eight¦352221 tables.
\v 42 There were four cut-stone tables for the burnt offerings, \add ≈each just under a metre long and wide, and standing half a metre tall\add*. They kept the tools which¦352247 they used to slaughter¦352248 the burnt offerings for the sacrifices¦352253 on¦352252 those \add stone\add* tables.
\v 43 Some two-pronged hooks¦352255 a handbreadth in length were fastened all around in the porch, and¦352255 the flesh¦352266 of the offerings¦352267 would be put on¦352259 the tables¦352265.
\s1 The priests' rooms
\rem /s1 Rooms for the Priests
\p
\v 44 Within the inner courtyard¦352274 \add but¦352269\add* on the outside¦352269 of the inner gate were singers' rooms¦352272—one on the northern side facing south, and¦352269 the other on the southern side facing north.
\v 45 Then¦352295 the man told me, “This room¦352298 facing south¦352302 is for the priests¦352303 who are on duty in the temple.
\v 46 The room¦352308 facing north¦352312 is for the priests¦352313 on duty at the altar¦352316, that¦352309 is, for Tsadok's¦352320 descendants—they're the \add only\add* Levites who approach¦352321 Yahweh¦352327 to serve¦352328 him¦352328.”
\s1 The inner courtyard
\rem /s1 The Inner Courtyard and the Temple Building; The Inner Courtyard and Temple
\p
\v 47 Next he measured¦352330 the square¦352341 courtyard¦352333 at fifty-four metres long and¦352330 wide¦352338, with the altar¦352342 in front¦352343 of the temple.
\s1 The temple entrance
\rem /s1 The Temple
\p
\v 48 Then¦352346 the man took me to the temple porch and measured¦352351 its doorposts—they were each two and a half metres thick. The entryway itself was seven metres¦352355 wide,\f + \fr 40:48 \ft Following the LXX, as the 1.5m (three cubits) in the Hebrew appears likely to be a copying mistake.\f* and the walls on each side of it were one and a half metres wide.
\v 49 The porch¦352370 was eleven metres wide and six metres deep, \add with¦352377\add* steps¦352377 going up to it and pillars¦352381 standing on¦352377 each side of it.
\c 41
\s1 Inside the temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦352390 the man took me into the temple's¦352393 holy place and¦352390 measured¦352394 the doorposts \add at\add* three metres¦352400 thick on each side.
\v 2 The doorway was five metres wide \add and\add* the wall on each side was two and a half metres long. Then¦352413 the man measured¦352425 the dimensions of the holy place—twenty-one metres long and eleven metres wide.
\v 3 Then¦352433 the man went¦352433 into the \add very sacred\add* inner¦352434 place and measured¦352435 the door at one metre wide, and the doorway was three metres wide. The walls on either side were nearly four metres wide.
\v 4 Then he measured¦352449 the square room's length¦352452 and¦352449 width¦352455 of eleven metres, \add measuring\add* to the temple¦352461 hallway. Then he said¦352462 to me, “This is the most sacred¦352465 area.”
\s1 The temple rooms
\rem /s1 The Rooms Built against the Temple Walls
\p
\v 5 Then¦352468 the man measured¦352468 the wall of the house at three metres thick. The width¦352474 of each side room around the house was two metres.
\v 6 There were side rooms on three¦352489 levels, one room above another, thirty¦352490 rooms on each level. There were ledges¦352492 around the temple wall to support all the side rooms, as the supports weren't built into the temple wall.
\v 7 So¦352510 the side rooms widened and went around going up, because¦352515 the house went around higher and higher all around—the rooms widened \add ≈on each higher level\add*, and a stairway went up through the middle¦352538 level to the highest level.
\v 8 Then¦352540 I saw¦352540 a raised part all around the temple, the foundation¦352546 for the side rooms¦352548. It measured a full rod in height¦352542—\add ≈just over three metres¦352552\add*.
\rem /s1 The Building on the West
\v 9 The width of the wall¦352556 of the side rooms on the outside was two and¦352565 a half metres¦352564. There was an open space¦352566 outside those rooms in the temple.
\v 10 On the other side of that open space were the priests' outer side rooms¦352573. That space was eleven metres wide all around the temple.
\v 11 There were doors into the side rooms from another open space¦352585 with one doorway¦352590 on the north¦352589 side, and¦352583 the other on the south¦352592. The width¦352593 of that open area was two and a half metres¦352597 all around.
\s1 The western side
\p
\v 12 The building that¦352603 faced the courtyard on the western side¦352608 was thirty-six metres wide. Its wall¦352615 was two and a half metres thick all around, and¦352602 it was forty-seven metres long.
\s1 The temple building measurements
\rem /s1 The Total Measurements of the Temple Building
\p
\v 13 Then¦352628 the man measured the temple as being fifty-four metres long. The separate¦352635 building¦352636, its¦352637 wall, and¦352628 the courtyard \add combined\add* also measured fifty-four metres \add ≈across\add*.
\v 14 The width¦352642 of the front of the courtyard in front of the temple was also fifty-four metres.
\p
\v 15 Then¦352650 the man measured¦352650 the length¦352651 of the building¦352653 behind¦352661 the temple¦352668, to its¦352662 west, and including¦352662 the galleries¦352662 on either side, it¦352661 came to fifty-four metres.
\rem /s1 Details of the Temple Building
\p The sacred inner¦352669 place¦352657 and the porch¦352670,
\v 16 the inner walls and the windows, including¦352674 the narrow¦352675 windows, and the galleries¦352676 all around on three¦352679 levels, were all panelled¦352682 in wood.
\v 17 Above the entryway to the inner¦352701 sanctuary and spaced along the walls there was a \add ≈repeating\add* pattern.
\v 18 It was decorated with winged creatures and palm trees¦352717—with a palm tree¦352718 between¦352719 each winged creature, and each winged creature had two¦352723 faces¦352724:
\v 19 the human¦352728 face looked toward a palm tree on one side, and¦352727 the young¦352735 lion face looked toward a palm tree on the other side. They were carved all around the entire¦352743 temple.
\v 20 From the ground¦352750 to above the doorway, winged creatures and palm¦352756 trees¦352756 were carved on the outer wall¦352758 of the temple¦352759.
\v 21 The door posts of the \add ≈main temple¦352762 room¦352766\add* were square¦352764, all with the same appearance.
\rem /s1 The Wooden Altar
\v 22 The wooden altar¦352770 was one metre on each side and stood one and a half metres¦352773 high¦352774. Its corner¦352779 posts, base, and frame were \add all\add* made of wood.
\p Then the man said to me, “This is the table¦352787 that¦352788 stands \add ≈in Yahweh's¦352790 presence\add*.”
\s1 The temple doorways
\rem /s1 The Doors
\p
\v 23 Both the main room¦352795 and¦352795 the sacred place¦352795 had double¦352792 doors¦352793—
\v 24 each consisting of two parts that swung on hinges.
\v 25 There were carvings of winged creatures and palm¦352817 trees¦352817 on the doors¦352814, just like on the walls¦352820, and there was a wooden roof over the front¦352825 porch¦352826.
\v 26 There were narrow windows¦352829 and palm¦352831 trees¦352831 on either side of the porch¦352837, like the side rooms¦352838 of the temple, and they also had overhanging roofs.
\c 42
\s1 Two buildings near the temple
\rem /s1 Rooms for the Priests; The Holy Chambers and the Outer Wall; Two Buildings Near the Temple
\p
\v 1 Next the man sent me out to the outer courtyard on the northern side, and he brought¦352850 me to rooms in front of the outer courtyard and the northern outer wall.
\v 2 That building with a north-facing entrance¦352872 was fifty-four metres long and¦352874 twenty-seven metres wide.
\v 3 Some of those rooms faced the inner¦352882 courtyard and were eleven metres away from the sanctuary. There were three¦352894 levels of rooms, and the ones above looked down on¦352894 the ones below and were open to them, having a walkway. Some of the rooms looked out onto the outer¦352887 courtyard.
\v 4 A five metre wide passage¦352898 ran in front¦352896 of the rooms and¦352908 was fifty-four\f + \fr 42:4 \ft The Hebrew text seemed to be missing a word here (as it didn't make sense), so the word from the (approx. 300 B.C.) Septuagint translation was used.\f* metres¦352900 long. The rooms' doors \add ≈faced\add* north¦352909.
\v 5 But¦352911 the upper¦352912 halls were smaller, because¦352914 the walkways \add ≈there used\add* more space than they¦352916 did in the bottom and¦352920 middle¦352920 levels of the building.
\v 6 \add @The third storey halls\add* had no pillars¦352929 (unlike the courtyards¦352930), so \add @the rooms at the highest level\add* were smaller than those on the bottom and middle¦352936 levels.
\v 7 The outside¦352942 wall ran along the rooms toward the outer¦352947 courtyard¦352946 that¦352940 was in front¦352950 of the rooms, \add and¦352939\add* was twenty-seven metres long¦352952.
\v 8 The¦352965 \add row of\add* rooms along the outer¦352962 courtyard¦352961 was twenty-seven metres long, and¦352965 the \add row of\add* rooms facing the sanctuary was fifty-four metres long.
\v 9 There was an entrance to the lowest rooms from the eastern¦352979 side, coming from the outer¦352983 courtyard¦352982.
\v 10 There was \add also a set of\add* rooms¦352999 along the outer courtyard wall on the eastern¦352990 side of the outer courtyard, in front¦352993 of the sanctuary's inner courtyard,
\v 11 with a walkway in front¦353002 of them. They \add ≈looked similar to\add* the rooms¦353004 on the northern¦353007 side and had the same \add ≈measurements\add*, as well as the same exits¦353012 and arrangements¦353013 and doors.
\v 12 There were also doorways into rooms on the south¦353020 side that¦353018 were similar¦353016 to what was on the north side. There was an inside passage with¦353022 an outer door—the passage had doors into all the rooms. At the eastern¦353029 end of the passage there was a door from the outside.
\p
\v 13 Then¦353032 the man told me, “The northern¦353035 rooms and the southern¦353037 rooms that¦353038 are in front¦353041 of the outer courtyard¦353042 are sacred rooms where¦353047 the priests¦353051 who work nearest to Yahweh¦353055 may eat the very sacred food. They'll put the very sacred things there—the food offering, the sin offering, and the guilt¦353065 offering—because¦353066 it's a very sacred area.
\v 14 When the priests¦353071 enter¦353070 it, they must not go¦353074 \add back\add* out of the sacred area to the outer¦353079 courtyard¦353078, without laying aside the clothes in which they served¦353085, since those \add ≡clothes\add* are sacred. So they must dress in other¦353094 clothes before going \add ≈back into the public areas\add*.”
\s1 Measuring outside the temple
\rem /s1 The Measurements of the Temple Area
\p
\v 15 The man completed¦353101 measuring the temple interior and then¦353101 took me out to the eastern¦353113 gate¦353109 and measured¦353114 all the surrounding area there.
\v 16-19 He measured¦353119 each side¦353120 \add of the wall\add* with¦353122 \add &his\add* measuring rod, \add and it was\add* 270m\f + \fr 42:16 \ft Following the Septuagint here, which seems more accurate (unless he now has a shorter measuring rod).\f* long on¦353122 each of the eastern¦353121, northern, southern, and western sides.
\v 20 \add ≈So\add* the four¦353168 sides¦353169 that were measured¦353170 (and thus the wall around it) was 270m on each side, and the wall separated¦353182 the sacred¦353184 area from the \add ≈public area\add*.
\c 43
\s1 The powerful splendour of God there to temple
\rem /s1 The Lord Returns to the Temple; The Divine Glory Returns to the Temple; The Lord's Glory Returns; The Glory Returns to the Temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦353187 the man took me to the gate that¦353192 opened to the east.
\v 2 Wow! The splendour¦353198 of Yisrael's¦353200 god¦353199 \add ≈appeared towards\add* the east. His voice¦353204 was like the \add ≈roar of rushing water¦353206\add*, and¦353197 the land shone with his \add ≈brilliance\add*.\x + \xo 43:2: \xt Eze 10:3-4,18-19; 11:22-23; Rev 1:15.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈What I saw in this vision was like what I'd seen in my earlier\add* visions¦353225, first by the Kebar¦353233 Canal, then¦353212 later when he\f + \fr 43:3 \ft It's only one letter difference, but the Hebrew has ‘I’ here which seems wrong and is adjusted in some later manuscripts.\f* came to destroy¦353221 \add @Yerushalem\add*. I fell to \add my knees with¦353220\add* my face¦353237 \add to the ground\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈So¦353239\add* Yahweh's¦353240 \add ≈brightness\add* entered into the temple via the eastern¦353250 entrance.
\p
\v 5 Then¦353252 the spirit¦353253 lifted¦353252 me up and brought¦353254 me into the inner¦353258 court¦353257, and wow, Yahweh's¦353263 brilliance was filling the temple.
\v 6 Then¦353266 as the man¦353270 was standing¦353272 \add there\add* beside¦353273 me, I heard¦353266 someone speak to me from inside the temple,
\v 7 and \add @the voice\add* told me, “Humanity's child, this is the place¦353311 \add where I'll sit on¦353308\add* my throne¦353283 and \add ≈rest\add* my feet¦353288—where¦353289 I'll live in¦353308 the middle¦353293 of the Israeli¦353294 people¦353294 forever¦353297. Yisrael's \add ≈descendants\add* and their kings will no longer profane my sacred¦353305 name with their faithlessness or with the corpses¦353309 of their kings at¦353308 their \add ≈hilltop¦353311 shrines¦353311\add*.
\v 8 They'll no longer profane my sacred name by putting their \add ≈entrance\add* next to my \add ≈entrance\add*, and their doorposts next to my doorposts, with nothing but¦353318 a wall between me and them. They profaned my sacred name with their disgusting actions, so I \add ≈destroyed¦353333\add* them with my anger¦353335.
\v 9 Now they're to remove their prostitution¦353341 and \add ?their kings' corpses¦353342\add* from in¦353346 front of me, and I'll¦353345 live \add ≈among¦353346 them\add* forever¦353347.
\p
\v 10 “Humanity's child, \add ≈describe that temple to the Israeli¦353357 people\add* so they'll be ashamed¦353363 of their¦353364 \add ≈disobedience\add* and¦353365 \add ?≈think about those plans\add*.
\v 11 If they're ashamed¦353372 of everything they've done¦353376, then reveal the design of that temple to them—its details, its entrances¦353381 and exits¦353380, its complete design, and all its decrees and rules. Then¦353370 \add right\add* \add ≈in front of them,\add* write¦353400 that down so they'll see its complete design and all its rules, and then obey them.
\v 12 This is the regulation for the temple: From the peak of the hill¦353422 to all the surrounding border around it, it'll be very sacred¦353429. Listen, that's the regulation for the temple.
\s1 The altar dimensions
\rem /s1 The Altar
\p
\v 13 These are the altar's measurements, using the same kind of measuring rod that was used for the temple area: There's a gutter¦353444 around the altar that's \add both\add* half a metre deep and¦353437 wide. There's a rim around it that's 23cm wide. That'll be the base for \add the rest of\add* the altar.\x + \xo 43:13-17: \xt Exo 27:1-2; 2Ch 4:1.\x*
\v 14 The lower part of the gutter¦353460 is one metre high to the lower ledge that surrounds the altar. The lower ledge is half a metre wide. From that ledge to the upper ledge, it's two¦353466 metres¦353467. That ledge is also half a metre wide.
\v 15 The altar¦353482 body \add at the top\add* is another two metres¦353484 high and¦353482 has a \add ≈hornlike projection\add* jutting up \add from each of its corners\add*.
\v 16 The sides¦353503 of the square¦353500 body are six metres long.
\v 17 The upper ledge¦353505 that borders¦353515 the altar body is also a square, eight metres long on¦353509 each side, and¦353505 has a rim all around that's 27cm wide. There's a gutter¦353520 measuring half a metre at¦353509 the bottom of the altar. There are steps¦353525 leading up to the east side of the altar.”
\s1 The purification of the new altar
\rem /s1 The Consecration of the Altar
\p
\v 18 Then¦353529 \add @the man\add* told me, “Humanity's child, the master¦353536 Yahweh¦353536 says¦353535 that those are the dimensions for when they make the altar¦353540 for presenting the burnt¦353545 offering¦353543 on, and¦353546 for sprinkling¦353546 blood¦353548 onto.\x + \xo 43:18-27: \xt Exo 29:35-37.\x*
\v 19 You must¦353550 give¦353550 a bull¦353565 from the cattle as a sin¦353569 offering¦353569 for the Levitical¦353554 priests¦353553 who are Tsadok's¦353558 descendants—those who approach me to serve¦353564 me. That's the master¦353562 Yahweh's¦353563 declaration.
\v 20 Then¦353571 you'll take¦353571 some of \add @the bull's\add* blood¦353572 and place it on the altar's ‘horns¦353577’ and on the four top edges and the surrounding rim. You'll purify¦353587 it and \add ≈make¦353589 it allowable in my presence\add*.
\v 21 Then¦353591 take¦353591 that sin¦353594 offering¦353594 bull¦353593 and¦353595 burn¦353595 it¦353595 in¦353596 the designated part of the temple area outside¦353598 the sanctuary¦353599.
\v 22 Then¦353601 on¦353601 the second¦353602 day¦353601, you'll get from the goats¦353606 a male goat¦353604 without any blemishes, and¦353601 offer it as a sin offering¦353608—the priests will purify the altar¦353612 just as they purified it with¦353601 the bull¦353615.
\v 23 When you finish¦353617 that purification¦353618, offer an unblemished bull¦353620 from the cattle and an unblemished ram from the flock¦353628,
\v 24 and offer¦353638 them to \add me,\add* Yahweh. The priests¦353635 will throw¦353634 salt onto them and present¦353631 them as a burnt¦353640 offering¦353640 to Yahweh.
\v 25 Then every day¦353649 for \add the next\add* seven days¦353644 you¦353645 must bring a billy goat¦353646 to the priest to be sacrificed for an offering¦353648, so I'll accept the altar. You must also sacrifice a young¦353651 bull¦353650 and a ram, each without any defect, that the priests will provide.
\v 26 They must atone¦353663 for the altar¦353666 for seven days¦353662 and purify¦353667 it, and in that way they must consecrate it \add to me\add*.
\v 27 \add ≈After doing all that\add*, from the eighth¦353680 day¦353679 onward¦353681 the priests¦353683 will prepare you \add people\add*'s burnt¦353689 offerings and your peace¦353692 offerings on¦353679 the altar¦353686, and I'll¦353693 accept¦353693 you¦353694. That's the master¦353696 Yahweh's¦353697 declaration.”
\c 44
\s1 The locked doorway
\rem /s1 The Prince, the Levites, the Priests; The Use of the East Gate; The Prince, Levites, and Priests; The Closed Gate
\p
\v 1 Then the man took me back to the outer¦353705 sanctuary¦353704 gate¦353703 facing east, \add ≈but¦353700\add* it was shut,
\v 2 and¦353711 Yahweh told me, “This gate¦353714 has been sealed shut—it won't¦353718 be opened¦353719. No person will go¦353723 through it, because¦353725 Yisrael's god¦353727 Yahweh entered through it, so it's \add now\add* closed.
\v 3 \add Only Yisrael's\add* ruler will sit by it to eat food¦353745 \add ≈in front¦353748 of\add* Yahweh¦353749, \add but¦353754\add* he'll enter via the gate's porch¦353751, and leave the same way.”
\s1 Foreigners disallowed in the temple
\rem /s1 Rules for Admission to the Temple; Admission to the Temple
\p
\v 4 Then¦353757 he took me to the front¦353764 of the temple by way of the northern¦353761 gate, and I looked¦353766, and wow, Yahweh's¦353771 brilliance filled¦353768 \add *his\add* temple, and I fell to \add my knees with\add* my face \add bowed towards the ground\add*.
\v 5 Then¦353781 Yahweh told me, “Humanity's child, \add ≈pay attention\add* and look¦353789 with your eyes¦353790 and listen¦353792 with your ears¦353791 to everything that I'm declaring to you: all the instructions¦353802 about Yahweh's¦353783 temple and all its regulations¦353802. \add ≈Take special note of\add* the temple's entrance¦353812 and exits¦353815.
\v 6 Then¦353818 tell the rebellious¦353821 \add ≈Israelis¦353824\add* that the master¦353828 Yahweh¦353828 says¦353827 this: Let all your¦353835 disgusting actions be enough¦353830 for you¦353832 Israeli¦353824 people—
\v 7 that you brought¦353839 foreigners¦353842 with uncircumcised hearts¦353845 and uncircumcised flesh¦353847 to be in¦353849 my sanctuary¦353849, profaning¦353850 my temple, while you \add people\add* were offering¦353854 me food, fat, and blood¦353859. You \add people\add* have broken¦353860 my agreement¦353863 with all your disgusting actions.
\v 8 You haven't carried out your duties regarding my sacred things¦353872, but¦353869 \add instead,\add* you \add people\add* appointed¦353873 others to take up your duties, and¦353873 you assigned them to care for my sacred place.
\p
\v 9 “The master¦353882 Yahweh¦353882 says¦353881 this: No foreigner, from any of those who live among the Israeli¦353906 people¦353905, may enter my sacred place if they're not physically circumcised or if they \add ≈haven't declared themself to be one of my people\add*.
\s1 Demotion of the Levites
\rem /s1 The Levites Are Excluded from the Priesthood
\p
\v 10 The¦353916 Levites¦353911 went far from me—wandering away from me \add and\add* going after¦353920 their idols¦353921, but¦353922 they'll be punished¦353923.
\v 11 They're \add still\add* servants in my sanctuary¦353926, watching the temple gates¦353931 and serving inside, and they slaughter¦353938 the burnt¦353941 offerings¦353941 and the people's sacrifices¦353944, and they'll stand¦353947 in front of the people and serve¦353949 them.
\v 12 \add However,\add* because they sacrificed to their idols¦353956, they became sinful stumbling¦353961 blocks¦353961 for the \add ≈Israeli¦353958 people\add*, therefore¦353963 I've \add ≈promised to take action\add* against them, and they'll \add ≈pay for their disobedience\add*. That's the master¦353970 Yahweh's¦353971 declaration.
\v 13 They mustn't come near to me to act as my priests¦353979 or to approach any of my sacred things, the most sacred things. Instead, they'll bear their shame¦353992 and¦353975 their guilt for the disgusting things they've done,
\v 14 but¦353997 I'll¦353997 place them as housekeepers¦354001 in the temple—for all its \add mundane\add* duties and¦353997 \add ≈all the menial work¦354003\add* that's done¦354006 in it.
\s1 The priests descendants of Saduk
\rem /s1 The Levitical Priests; The Priests
\p
\v 15 Then the Levitical¦354011 priests¦354010, those descendants of Tsadok¦354013 who fulfilled the duties of my sanctuary¦354019 when the Israeli¦354021 people¦354021 were wandering away from me, they'll approach me to worship me. They'll stand¦354029 in¦354020 front of me to offer¦354031 me the fat and the blood¦354034. That's the master¦354036 Yahweh's¦354037 declaration.
\v 16 They'll come¦354040 to my sanctuary¦354043 and approach my table¦354048 to worship me and to fulfill their duties to me.
\v 17 When¦354055 they come to the gates of the inner courtyard, they'll have to dress in linen¦354063 clothing¦354062, because they mustn't \add ≈wear\add* wool inside¦354074 the courtyard gates or in the temple.\x + \xo 44:17-18: \xt Exo 28:39-43; Lev 16:4.\x*
\v 18 \add ≈They must¦354078 wear\add* linen turbans on¦354090 their heads¦354081 and linen underclothes¦354082 around their waists¦354087. They mustn't dress in¦354090 clothing that makes them sweat¦354090.
\v 19 When¦354092 they go \add back\add* out to the outer courtyard¦354095 to go \add back again\add* to the people, they must take off the clothes they wore when they served—they must take them off and¦354092 lay them down in¦354092 a sacred room, so they don't¦354120 make other¦354119 people sacred by contact with¦354092 their special clothing.\x + \xo 44:19: \xt Lev 16:23.\x*
\p
\v 20 \add @The priests\add* mustn't shave¦354130 their heads or allow their hair to hang loosely, but¦354128 they must¦354130 \add ≈keep the hair on their heads neatly trimmed\add*.
\v 21 No priest¦354146 should drink¦354143 wine \add ≈before\add* he comes to the inner¦354151 court¦354150.\x + \xo 44:21: \xt Lev 10:9.\x*
\v 22 \add ≈Also¦354166, priests can't¦354155 marry widows of men who weren't priests, or women who've been divorced¦354154—they're permitted to marry only Israeli¦354165 women who've never been married, or widows of other priests\add*.
\p
\v 23 They must¦354177 teach the people¦354176 the difference between things that are sacred¦354179 and things that are not, and teach them how to know what things are acceptable to me and what things are not.\x + \xo 44:23: \xt Lev 10:10.\x*
\v 24 In a dispute¦354189 they must judge¦354192 in¦354194 accordance with¦354194 my instructions¦354203 and regulations¦354203. They must keep my law and my statutes at¦354194 every religious celebration, and they must observe¦354207 my sacred rest days.
\p
\v 25 \add @Priests\add* mustn't defile themselves by going near a person's dead body. However, if the dead person was his father¦354223 or mother¦354224, son or daughter¦354226, or brother¦354227 or unmarried sister¦354228, then¦354213 they can defile themselves,
\v 26 and¦354237 after¦354237 they've been purified¦354238, they must¦354241 \add ≈wait\add* for seven¦354239 days¦354240.
\v 27 Then¦354245 \add after that\add*, \add ≈when\add* he goes¦354246 into the inner¦354253 courtyard¦354252 to serve¦354254 in¦354245 the sacred¦354249 place, he must¦354256 bring¦354256 a sin¦354257 offering¦354257 for himself. That's the master¦354259 Yahweh's¦354260 declaration.
\p
\v 28 \add ≈The priests must¦354262 not own¦354267 any land\add*—I am their inheritance and¦354262 I'll be their property.\x + \xo 44:28: \xt Num 18:20.\x*
\v 29 They'll have the food offerings, the sin¦354277 offerings, and the guilt¦354278 offerings to eat¦354280, plus¦354277 everything in¦354284 Yisrael¦354284 that's devoted¦354283 to Yahweh will be theirs.\x + \xo 44:29-30: \xt Num 18:8-19.\x*
\v 30 The best of the firstfruits \add ≈from every harvest\add* and every contribution¦354295 \add from the people\add* will belong¦354300 to the priests, and you \add Israelis\add* will give the first of your \add bread\add* dough¦354302\f + \fr 44:30 \ft Probably after it's cooked???\f* to the priests so that blessing¦354306 may rest on your home.
\v 31 Priests must¦354322 not eat¦354322 \add ≈the meat of any bird or animal that's found dead or that's been killed by any other\add* animal.\x + \xo 44:31: \xt Lev 22:8.\x*
\c 45
\s1 Yahweh's section of land
\rem /s1 Division of the Land; The Holy District; The Lord's Portion of the Country
\p
\v 1 When you throw dice to divide up the land as an inheritance¦354330 \add for the tribes\add*, you must¦354331 make an offering of land to Yahweh¦354333. That offering will become a sacred part of the land: \add ≈thirteen kilometres by ten kilometres in¦354326 size\add*.\f + \fr 45:1 \ft The ‘ten kilometres’ (twenty thousand cubits) from the Septuagint appears to correct the Hebrew which has half of that, but then v3 and v4 wouldn't make sense.\f* \add ≈That entire¦354350 area\add* will become sacred.
\v 2 \add ≈Within that area,\add* a 270m wide square¦354364 area that'll surround the sacred¦354359 place¦354359 \add ≈will be left empty\add*, with¦354362 a further border around it twenty-seven metres wide.
\v 3 Inside that \add first, larger\add* area, measure¦354376 a \add rectangle\add* thirteen kilometres by five¦354378 kilometres for the sanctuary¦354388—the most sacred¦354389 place¦354389.
\v 4 It'll be a sacred place in the nation for the priests¦354397 who serve Yahweh, who come near Yahweh to serve him. It'll be a place for their houses¦354409 and a sacred area for the sacred place.
\v 5 The \add other half\add*, which is \add also\add* thirteen kilometres long and¦354413 five¦354413 kilometres wide, will be for towns for the Levites¦354422 who serve¦354423 in the temple.
\p
\v 6 You'll designate an area thirteen kilometres long by half a kilometre wide for the city that will be next to the area reserved for the holy place. This city will belong¦354447 to all the \add ≈Israeli¦354445 people\add*.
\s1 The leader's section of land
\rem /s1 Land for the Prince
\p
\v 7 The leader's land will be on both sides of the area reserved for the holy place and the city—on the west and the east. Its length¦354475 will \add ≈be the same as those portions¦354478 that it's parallel¦354476 to\add*.
\v 8 That area will belong¦354487 to Yisrael's leader \add so\add* the leaders won't¦354492 keep oppressing¦354494 my people¦354499, \add ≈but¦354492 rather\add* they'll give¦354501 the rest of the land¦354486 to \add ≈be divided among the tribes¦354505\add*.
\rem /s1 Rules for the Prince
\v 9 The master Yahweh says this: \add ≈That's plenty\add* for you, leaders of Yisrael¦354517! \add ≈Replace your\add* violence¦354519 and strife \add ≈with\add* justice¦354521 and righteousness¦354522. Quit your evictions¦354525 of my people¦354527. That's the master Yahweh's¦354512 declaration.
\s1 Dealing with produce and animals
\rem /s1 Weights and Measures
\p
\v 10 You \add Israelis\add* must \add ≈use accurate scales and measuring containers\add*.\x + \xo 45:10: \xt Lev 19:36.\x*
\v 11 \add ≈The baskets for measuring dry things and containers¦354549 for measuring liquids (called an ‘efah’ for dry things and a ‘bath’ for liquids, both a tenth of the large 220 litre ‘homer’) must be the same volume—each must hold twenty-two litres\add*.
\v 12 \add ≈For weights, the shekel weight must¦354563 be ten¦354570 grams. (Twenty ‘gerahs¦354564’ makes one shekel, then¦354562 sixty shekels makes one ‘mina’.)\add*
\rem /s1 Special Offerings and Celebrations; Offerings; Offerings and Holy Days
\p
\v 13 You \add Israelis\add* must¦354578 present \add to the leader\add* \add ≈one measure of wheat¦354584 or barley¦354588 for every sixty measures\add* \add that¦354579 you¦354580 harvest\add*,
\v 14 \add ≈one measure of olive¦354593 oil for every one hundred measures\add* \add that you produce\add*,
\v 15 plus¦354620 one sheep¦354608 or goat from the flock¦354613 for every two¦354616 hundred¦354616 animals from the watered regions¦354617 of Yisrael¦354618, to be used for any burnt¦354620 offering or peace¦354621 offering to make atonement¦354622 for the people. That's the master¦354625 Yahweh's¦354626 declaration.
\p
\v 16 All the people¦354629 in¦354637 the country will give that contribution¦354634 to Yisrael's leader.
\v 17 It'll be the leader's responsibility to supply the animals for the burnt offerings, the grain offerings, and the drink¦354645 offerings at¦354646 the festivals and the new moon celebrations, and on¦354646 the rest days—all the \add ≈Israeli people's\add* scheduled festivals. He'll provide¦354656 for the sin offerings, the grain offerings, the burnt offerings, and the peace¦354668 offerings \add ≈to make the Israeli people right\add* \add with¦354646 God\add*.
\s1 The festivals
\rem /s1 Festivals; The Festivals
\r (Exo. 12:1-20; Lev. 23:33-43)
\p
\v 18 The master¦354679 Yahweh¦354679 says¦354678 this: On the first \add day¦354682\add* of the first month¦354683 \add of the year\add*, you¦354684 must¦354684 take¦354684 an unblemished¦354690 bull¦354685 from the herd and¦354691 perform a sin¦354691 offering \add to purify¦354691\add* the temple.
\v 19 The priest¦354697 will take¦354696 some of the blood¦354698 of that sin¦354699 offering¦354699 and place it on the doorposts of the temple and on the four¦354707 corners¦354708 of the border of the altar¦354710, and on the doorposts of the gate¦354714 to the inner¦354716 court¦354715.
\v 20 You'll do¦354719 this \add again\add* on¦354720 the seventh¦354720 \add day¦354720\add* of that month¦354721 for each person's¦354724 accidental sin, or sin by ignorance¦354724. \add In that way you'll\add* \add ≈make¦354725 the temple pure\add* \add in¦354720 God's sight\add*.
\p
\v 21 Then on¦354731 the fourteenth day of that first¦354730 month¦354734, you \add Israelis\add* will have a seven-day celebration, \add ≈when you'll eat bread¦354741 made without any raising agent\add*.\x + \xo 45:21: \xt Exo 12:1-20; Num 28:16-25.\x*
\v 22 That same day¦354746, the leader will prepare a bull¦354754 as a sin¦354755 offering¦354755 for himself¦354748 and¦354744 for all the people¦354752 in the country.
\v 23 For the seven days of the celebration, the leader will prepare a burnt¦354762 offering for Yahweh¦354763: Seven bulls¦354765 and seven unblemished¦354768 rams each day for seven days, and a male goat each day as a sin offering.
\v 24 Then¦354777 the leader will perform a food offering¦354777 of twenty-two litres \add of flour\add* with seven litres of \add olive\add* oil¦354783 for each bull¦354779 and¦354777 each ram.
\p
\v 25 On the fifteenth day¦354790 of the seventh month¦354791 \add of the year\add*, at¦354788 the \add ‘temporary shelters’\add* celebration, the leader will perform offerings on¦354788 those seven¦354795 days¦354796: Sin offerings, burnt¦354798 offerings, food offerings, and offerings of oil¦354800.\x + \xo 45:25: \xt Lev 23:33-36; Num 29:12-38.\x*
\c 46
\s1 The leader and the festivals
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Regulations; The Prince and the Festivals
\p
\v 1 The master¦354806 Yahweh¦354806 says this: The eastern¦354812 gate of the inner¦354810 courtyard¦354809 will be shut for the six days of work, but¦354818 it'll be opened on rest days and on the day of the new moon.
\v 2 The leader will enter¦354825 the outer courtyard by the path¦354827 to that gate and its outside¦354830 porch¦354828, and he'll stand¦354831 at the doorposts¦354834 of the inner gate while the priests¦354837 perform his burnt¦354840 offering and peace¦354843 offering. Then¦354825 he'll worship¦354844 \add ≈right there\add* at the inner gate and \add ≈then¦354825 leave again\add*, but¦354825 the gate won't¦354851 be shut until¦354854 evening¦354856.
\v 3 The people¦354859 will also worship¦354858 in¦354865 front¦354867 of Yahweh¦354868 at¦354865 the entrance¦354862 to that gate¦354863 on¦354865 rest days and¦354858 new moons¦354866.
\v 4 The burnt¦354870 offering¦354870 that¦354871 the leader offers to Yahweh¦354875 on the rest day¦354876 will be six¦354878 unblemished lambs¦354879 and¦354881 an unblemished ram.
\v 5 The grain offering that he gives with the ram is twenty-two litres \add of flour\add*, and¦354884 with the lambs¦354887 it should be as much as he pleases, along with one litre of \add olive\add* oil for every twenty-two litres of flour.
\v 6 On the day¦354895 of the new moon¦354896 he must¦354906 offer a bull¦354897, six¦354902 lambs¦354903, and¦354895 a ram—\add ≈all without any defects\add*.
\v 7 He must¦354912 \add also\add* provide¦354912 a grain¦354913 offering¦354913 of twenty-two litres of flour with the bull¦354909, the same with the ram, and¦354908 as much as he¦354912 wants with the lambs¦354914, along with a litre of \add olive\add* oil¦354918 with every twenty-two litres of flour.
\v 8 The leader enters¦354922 by the path to the gate¦354926 and its porch¦354925, and he must¦354927 leave by the same path.
\p
\v 9 But¦354931 when the land's people¦354932 come in¦354931 front¦354935 of Yahweh¦354936 at¦354931 the scheduled¦354937 festivals, anyone entering through the north gate to worship¦354943 must leave through the south gate, and¦354931 anyone entering through the south gate must leave through the north gate. No one is allowed to turn back to the gate through which¦354963 they entered, because¦354967 everyone¦354932 must keep going straight ahead until they get out.
\v 10 The leader must be amongst¦354973 them in their¦354973 midst¦354973—when they go in, he must go in, and¦354972 when they leave, he must leave.
\v 11 During the festivals¦354979 \add that you hold for me\add*, the king must¦354981 present twenty-two litres of flour along with a bull¦354984 or a ram, and¦354979 as much flour as he wants to with the lambs¦354987, along with a litre of \add olive\add* oil¦354990 with every twenty-two litres of flour.
\p
\v 12 When the leader gives a voluntary offering to Yahweh¦355005 (either a burnt offering or a peace offering), the eastern¦355012 gate will be opened¦355006 for him. He'll offer his burnt offering or his peace offering just like he does on the rest day¦355022. Then he must¦354997 go out, and after¦355029 he's gone out, the gate will be shut.
\s1 The daily sacrifice
\rem /s1 The Daily Offering
\p
\v 13 In addition¦355032, \add ≈every morning someone\add*\f + \fr 46:13 \ft Some of the earliest translations (the Septuagint and the Vulgate) have ‘he’ here, suggesting it must be the leader.\f* \add ≈must provide\add* a one year¦355035 old lamb¦355032 with¦355041 no¦355036 defects¦355036 to be \add ≈completely burnt¦355038 up as an\add* offering¦355038 to Yahweh¦355040.
\v 14 \add Also¦355061\add* \add ≈every morning\add* someone must¦355047 provide an offering¦355047 of four litres of flour¦355059 mixed with¦355049 a litre of \add olive\add* oil¦355053, \add and¦355053\add* \add ≈that's a permanent¦355063 requirement\add*.
\v 15 They'll prepare the lamb¦355070, the grain¦355073 offering, and¦355066 the oil¦355076, \add ≈every morning\add*—a permanent \add daily\add* burnt¦355079 offering.
\s1 The leader and the country
\rem /s1 The Prince and the Land
\p
\v 16 The master¦355086 Yahweh¦355086 says this: If the leader gives a gift¦355092 to any of his sons, it becomes his inheritance—that is, it'll become his sons' property¦355099 \add because¦355088\add* it's an inheritance.
\v 17 \add ≈But¦355103\add* if he gives a gift¦355106 from his inheritance to one of his servants¦355109, then it'll belong¦355122 to that servant until¦355112 the year¦355114 of liberty¦355115, and¦355103 then it'll return¦355116 to the leader. His inheritance must¦355105 definitely be for his \add own\add* sons¦355120.\x + \xo 46:17: \xt Lev 25:10.\x*
\v 18 The leader mustn't take the people's inheritance away from them, evicting them from their own property—he must¦355126 provide for his sons from his own property so that my people won't¦355124 be \add ≈forced off their own inherited land\add*.”
\s1 The temple kitchens
\rem /s1 The Temple Kitchens
\p
\v 19 Next¦355162 the man brought¦355146 me through the entrance¦355147 at¦355147 the gate¦355152 to the sacred¦355156 rooms¦355155 for the priests¦355159 which¦355148 were north-facing, and¦355162 what's more, there¦355164 was a place¦355165 toward¦355168 the west.
\v 20 He told me, “That's the place¦355174 where the priests¦355179 must boil the guilt¦355182 offering and the sin¦355185 offering and where they must bake the grain¦355190 offering. They mustn't bring the offerings into the outer¦355196 courtyard¦355195, because then the people¦355200 would become sacred¦355197 \add (and not be able to leave)\add*.”
\p
\v 21 Then¦355202 he brought¦355202 me to the outer¦355206 courtyard and led me past the four corners¦355211 of that courtyard, and I¦355213 saw that in¦355215 every corner of the courtyard there was \add another\add* \add ≈enclosed area\add*,
\v 22 and each was twenty-two metres long and¦355228 sixteen metres wide.
\v 23 Around the inside of each of those enclosed areas was a stone ledge, with cooking hearths¦355241 \add all around\add* under¦355243 the ledge.
\v 24 The man told me, “Those are the places where¦355252 the temple workers will boil the people's¦355261 sacrifices¦355260.”
\c 47
\s1 The healing river
\rem /s1 The Stream Flowing from the Temple; The River From the Temple; The River of Healing; Water Flowing from the Temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦355263 the man took me back to the temple entrance¦355266, and there was water flowing out from under the \add ≈front¦355278 edge of the building\add* toward¦355275 the east, and the water was flowing down the south side¦355284 of the temple, to the right of the altar¦355288.\x + \xo 47:1: \xt Zech 14:8; Yhn 7:38; Rev 22:1.\x*
\v 2 So¦355290 he took me out through the northern¦355294 gate and led me around¦355295 \add the outside¦355297\add* to the eastern¦355304 gate, and look, the water¦355307 was flowing from this gate on its south¦355312 side.
\v 3 As the man¦355316 \add ≈started walking\add* toward the east holding his measuring line in¦355322 his hand¦355319, he measured¦355320 off five hundred metres¦355322 and led me through the water¦355324, which was ankle-deep.
\v 4 Then¦355328 he measured another five hundred metres and led me through¦355336 the water¦355331, which was \add now\add* knee-deep, and then¦355328 after another five hundred metres it was \add ≈up to the waist\add*.
\v 5 Finally¦355346 he measured¦355340 off another five hundred metres, but¦355340 the water had become deep enough to swim in and¦355340 too deep to cross¦355347—it was \add now\add* a river that¦355343 couldn't be walked through¦355358.
\v 6 The man \add ≈asked\add* me, “Humanity's child, \add ≈have you taken note of all\add* this?”
\p Then¦355360 he led¦355366 me out and¦355360 had me \add ≈return\add* along the riverbank¦355369.
\v 7 As I walked back, wow, \add now\add* the riverbank¦355376 had many trees on each side.
\v 8 The man told me, “This water \add ≈flows eastward and down into the Rift Valley\add*. \add *It'll\add* flow into the Dead Sea and will make it into fresh¦355402 water.
\v 9 \add ≈As a result,\add* every kind of living¦355410 creature¦355409 that swarms¦355414 will live where that water flows. There'll be lots of fish¦355424 when that water flows \add @into the Dead Sea\add* as it causes the salty water to become¦355432 fresh¦355432 water—everything will live wherever that river¦355439 goes.
\v 10 Then¦355441 the fishermen¦355445 from Eyn-Gedi will stand \add there\add* by the water, and¦355441 there'll be a place¦355452 to dry out the fishing nets near Eyn-Eglaim. There'll be many kinds¦355455 of fish in the Dead Sea—plentiful just like the fish in the Mediterranean Sea.
\v 11 \add However,\add* the Dead Sea's swamps and marshes¦355466 won't¦355467 be made fresh—they'll be for providing salt.
\v 12 Beside that river¦355474 on both banks¦355478, all kinds of fruit trees will grow. Their leaves will never wilt, and \add ≈they'll never fail to fruit\add*—\add in fact\add* \add *they'll\add* bear fruit every¦355483 month¦355496 because¦355498 that water¦355499 from the temple flows to them. Their fruit will be \add ≈good to eat\add*, and their¦355499 leaves will \add ≈help with\add* healing¦355510.\x + \xo 47:12: \xt Rev 22:2.\x*
\s1 Land boundaries
\rem /s1 Division of the land among the tribes; Boundaries for the Land; The New Boundaries of the Land; The Boundaries of the Land
\p
\v 13 The master¦355515 Yahweh¦355515 says¦355514 that this will be the way that you¦355520 divide the land up for Yisrael's twelve tribes¦355526: Yosef¦355528 \add (Joseph)\add* will have \add ≈a double allocation\add*.
\v 14 You're to equally divide¦355531 \add the land\add* that I \add ≈took action\add* to give¦355540 to your¦355541 ancestors¦355541—\add from now on¦355546\add* that land will come to you¦355545 \add Israelis\add* as an inheritance¦355546.
\p
\v 15 \add #These will be the land boundaries¦355549\add*: On the north¦355552 side¦355551, it'll extend from the Mediterranean Sea east along the road towards Hetlon, and¦355548 then¦355548 on to Tsedad¦355560,
\v 16 to Berotah¦355564, and then on to Sivrayim, which is on the border between Damascus¦355570 and Hamat¦355573. The boundary will run as far as Hatser-Hattikon, which is on the border of the Havran \add region\add*.
\v 17 \add ≈So¦355582\add* the boundary will extend from the Mediterranean Sea to Hatsar-Enan on the border¦355589 between Hamat¦355595 to the north and¦355582 Damascus¦355590 \add to the south\add*. \add ≈That'll be the northern boundary\add*.
\p
\v 18 On the eastern side, the boundary will extend between Havran and Damascus¦355606, south along the Yordan¦355612 \add River\add* between the Gilead¦355608 \add region¦355610\add* and Yisrael's¦355611 territory¦355610, along the Dead Sea as far as Tamar.\f + \fr 47:18 \ft Tamar: the Hebrew has ‘the Eastern Sea’.\f* \add ≈That'll be the eastern boundary\add*.
\p
\v 19 On the southern side, the boundary will extend from Tamar¦355626 to the springs near Merivat-Kadesh¦355631. Then¦355623 it'll extend \add west\add* along the brook of Egypt to the Mediterranean Sea. \add ≈That'll be the southern boundary\add*.
\p
\v 20 On the western side, the boundary¦355648 will be the Mediterranean Sea, \add north\add* to a point near Levo-Hamat¦355653.
\p
\v 21 This is how you'll divide¦355659 this land for yourselves among Yisrael's tribes¦355665:
\v 22 You must assign the land as a permanent possession for yourselves and also for any foreigners who are living and raising their children¦355679 among you. You must consider them to be like native¦355683 born Israelis¦355684, and they must be assigned land among Yisrael's tribes¦355690.
\v 23 Wherever foreigners are living, you¦355701 must¦355701 give¦355701 them some land \add ≈to belong to them permanently\add*. That's the master¦355704 Yahweh's¦355705 declaration.”
\c 48
\s1 Land allocated to Yisrael's tribes
\rem /s1 Division of the Land; The Division of the Land; The Division of the Land among the Tribes; The Tribal Portions
\p
\v 1 Here's a list of Yisrael's tribes and the territory that each tribe is to receive. The northern boundary of Yisrael will start at the Mediterranean Sea and go \add east\add* to Hetlon \add city\add*, then to Levo-Hamat, and farther on to Hatsar-Enan¦355724, which is \add south\add* of Damascus¦355726, and it'll continue to Hamat. Each tribe will receive land that will extend from the eastern¦355737 boundary of Yisrael west to the Mediterranean Sea.
\rem /s1 The Special Section in the Center of the Land
\p The tribe of Dan¦355739 will receive land alongside Yisrael's northern border¦355725.
\v 2 \add ≈South¦355745 of their area\add* will be the territory for the tribe of Asher¦355753.
\v 3 \add ≈South of\add* Asher's¦355759 land will be territory for the tribe of Naftali¦355767.
\v 4 \add ≈South of\add* Naftali's¦355773 land will be territory for the tribe of Menashsheh.
\v 5 \add ≈South of\add* Menashsheh's land will be territory for the tribe of Efrayim¦355795.
\v 6 \add ≈South of\add* Efrayim's¦355801 land will be territory for the tribe of Reuven.
\v 7 \add ≈South of\add* Reuven's land will be territory for the tribe of Yehudah¦355823 \add (Judah)\add*.
\s1 The chosen place
\p
\v 8 \add ≈South of\add* Yehudah's¦355828 land will be an area that¦355838 the entire¦355840 nation will give to me—you \add Israelis\add* will set it apart for special use. The temple will be in¦355857 the middle¦355857 of the area that¦355838's thirteen kilometres wide and¦355826 as long as the other land allocations.
\p
\v 9 The special¦355859 area that¦355860 you \add Israelis\add* will give to Yahweh¦355862 will be thirteen kilometres long and five¦355864 kilometres wide.
\v 10 This special area will be allocated to the priests¦355876: you'll assign them an area of land measuring thirteen kilometres on the northern¦355877 and southern¦355889 sides, and five kilometres on the western¦355881 and eastern¦355885 sides. Yahweh's¦355897 temple will be in¦355898 the middle¦355898 of that special area.
\v 11 The temple area will be for the priests¦355900, Tsadok's¦355903 descendants, who are dedicated to serving me. (They're the ones who served me faithfully and didn't¦355908 turn away \add from me\add* like the Levites¦355916 \add ≈and the others\add* did.)
\v 12 The \add ≈priests' allocation\add* will be a portion¦355922 of that very sacred¦355922 land, extending to the Levites' border¦355928.
\v 13 The land assigned to the Levites will be the same size as the land that the priests¦355934 will receive. (So¦355931 together, these two portions of land will be thirteen kilometres long and¦355931 about ten kilometres wide.)
\v 14 They mustn't sell or barter it—none of this best part of Yisrael's land must¦355954 ever be \add ≈used for any other purpose\add*, because¦355963 it's all sacred¦355965 to Yahweh¦355966.
\p
\v 15 Another strip of land thirteen kilometres long and almost three kilometres wide will be allotted for other people living¦355982 in the special area to use. They may build homes and have pasturelands there, and there'll be a city in the middle¦355986 of that¦355970 area.
\v 16 The city \add ≈will be square, over two kilometres long on each side\add*,
\v 17 with an open¦356021 area around it¦356020, within the special area, that will be about 130m wide in each direction.
\v 18 Outside the city, there'll be two strips that'll extend five kilometres on¦356037 each of the east and west sides, and the produce¦356053 from \add @those strips\add* will be food for the people working in¦356037 the city.
\v 19 \add ≈Those¦356064 who come from the various tribes¦356063 to work in the city¦356060 may also work in this farmland\add*.
\v 20 That entire¦356076 special area, including¦356070 the land given for Yahweh's use and the city¦356084, will be a square that's thirteen kilometres long on¦356072 each side.
\p
\v 21 The tracts of land to the east and to the west of Yahweh's area and the city¦356096 will belong to the leader. One area will extend thirteen kilometres east to Yisrael's eastern¦356108 boundary, and the other will extend thirteen kilometres west \add to the Mediterranean Sea\add*. Yahweh's area, which¦356086 contains the temple, will be in¦356128 the middle¦356128.
\rem /s1 Land for the Other Tribes
\v 22 The¦356132 area that belongs¦356137 to the leader (that surrounds the Levites¦356132' property and the city¦356134 in the middle¦356135 of it) will be between the tribe of Yehudah¦356142 \add to the north\add* and the tribe of Benyamin¦356145 \add to the south\add*.
\p
\v 23 South of Yahweh's area, each of the other tribes will receive one section of land that extends from the eastern¦356152 \add boundary of Yisrael\add* west \add all the way to the Mediterranean Sea.\add*
\p Just south of Yahweh's area will be territory for the tribe of Benyamin¦356158.
\v 24 \add ≈South of\add* Benyamin's¦356164 land will be the land for the tribe of Shimeon.
\v 25 \add ≈South of\add* Shimeon's land will be the land for the tribe of Yissakar.
\v 26 \add ≈South of\add* Yissakar's land will be the land for the tribe of Zevulun.
\v 27 \add ≈South of\add* Zevulun's land will be the land for the tribe of Gad¦356214.
\v 28 Gad's¦356219 southern boundary will extend from Tamar¦356227 \add south\add* to the waters¦356228 of Merivah-Kadesh¦356230, and farther \add west\add* to the brook \add of Egypt\add*, and then¦356217 on to the Mediterranean Sea.
\p
\v 29 That \add describes\add* the territory you'll¦356241 allocate to Yisrael's tribes¦356243 to become their¦356246 permanent inheritance¦356242. That's the master¦356248 Yahweh's¦356249 declaration.
\s1 The city gateways
\rem /s1 The Gates of the City; The Gates of Jerusalem
\p
\v 30 These will be the exits¦356253 from the city¦356254: On the northern¦356256 side¦356255 (which will be just over two kilometres long),\x + \xo 48:30-34: \xt Rev 21:12-13.\x*
\v 31 there'll be three¦356271 gates named after the tribes¦356268 of Reuven, Yehudah¦356277, and¦356263 Levi¦356280.
\v 32 On the eastern¦356286 side¦356285 (the same length) there'll be three¦356292 gates¦356291 named after the tribes of Yosef¦356294, Benyamin¦356297, and¦356283 Dan¦356300.
\v 33 On the southern¦356305 side¦356303 (the same length) there'll be three¦356312 gates¦356311 named after the tribes of Shimeon, Yissakar, and¦356303 Zevulun.
\v 34 On the western side¦356323 (the same length) there'll be three¦356331 gates¦356330 named after the tribes of Gad¦356333, Asher¦356336, and Naftali¦356339.
\v 35 The \add ≈distance around the city¦356348\add* will be nine kilometres.
\p From that time onwards, the city's name¦356346 will be ‘Yahweh is there¦356352’ \add (Yahweh-Shammah)\add*.
